.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: This is the auxiliary blueprint `lem:claim-17-1-direct-real`, supporting paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: `claim:17-1`, lines 1140--1145 of the cited mirror.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: **Scope restriction:** The public statement uses `SubLineWitness` and real\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: parts. It does not prove the source distribution transport or the complex\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: modulus bound. These obligations and their discharge plan are recorded in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: `docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility.tex`, section\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: \\\"Scalar estimates on the auxiliary subline law\\\". -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: /-- On the directly indexed subline law, the real part of the `Z`-point\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: correlation is close to one with the fourth-root point and line error.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: This is the auxiliary blueprint `lem:claim-17-3-direct-real`, supporting paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: `claim:17-3`, lines 1204--1209 of the cited mirror.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: **Scope restriction:** This theorem retains the `SubLineWitness` carrier and\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: real-part conclusion. Transport to the source distribution remains open, as\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: recorded in `docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility.tex`, section\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: \\\"Scalar estimates on the auxiliary subline law\\\"; the source-labelled claim is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Claims.lean: not certified by this auxiliary result. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Defs.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary for `def:ith-restricted-line`: whenever a line\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Defs.lean: CL map keeps the shared scalar coordinate, the pre-decoding law it generates\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Defs.lean: against the point map gives positive mass to every coordinate-index event.  The\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Defs.lean: witness is the constant seed reconstructed from the index by\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Defs.lean: `seedOfIndexResidue`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary for item 3 of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines.lean: `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`: a consistency defect of two complete\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines.lean: measurements placed on opposite registers inflates by at most `4m^2` when\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines.lean: both line-point coordinates are restricted.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines.lean: `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1058-1061`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: # Positivity of the consistency defect on opposite placements\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: The error-inflation estimates for restricted line distributions apply to\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: nonnegative averages.  This module supplies the missing nonnegativity: the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: integrand of `consistencyDefect` for two complete measurements placed on\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: opposite registers is a state quadratic form of a positive operator.  Indeed,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: after separating the two placements along the corresponding tensor\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: bipartition, the off-diagonal sum is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: `∑_a (place p₁ E_a) (place p₂ (∑_{b ≠ a} F_b))`, a sum of products of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: positive operators supported on complementary registers.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: The consistency defect is blueprint `def:consistency`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:232-248`.  The\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: placements and their bipartitions are blueprint `def:expanded-state`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:420-450`.  The\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: nonnegativity is the hypothesis of items 1 and 2 of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1052-1058`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-- The state quadratic form of the zero operator vanishes. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-- Reindexing an operator along an equivalence preserves positivity.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: Formalization-only auxiliary for the placement bipartitions of blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: `def:expanded-state`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:420-450`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-- A register placement respects finite sums of operators. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-- A register placement maps the zero operator to zero. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-- Operators placed on `AA'` and `BA''` commute. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-- Operators placed on `AB''` and `BB'` commute. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-- Positive operators placed on `AA'` and on `BA''` have a positive product.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: Paper `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:420-450`,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: blueprint `def:expanded-state`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-- Positive operators placed on `AB''` and on `BB'` have a positive product.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: Paper `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:420-450`,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: blueprint `def:expanded-state`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-- Positive operators placed on a directed opposite pair of registers have a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: positive product.  Paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:420-450`,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: blueprint `def:expanded-state`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-! ## Nonnegativity of the pointwise consistency defect -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-- The off-diagonal outcome sum of two complete measurements placed on\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: opposite registers is a positive operator.  Formalization-only auxiliary for\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: blueprint `def:consistency`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:232-248`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: /-- The integrand of the consistency defect of two complete measurements placed\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: on opposite registers is nonnegative.  This is the positivity hypothesis needed\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: by items 1 and 2 of blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/ConsistencyPositivity.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1052-1058`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: # Error inflation of restricted line-point averages\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: This module records how a nonnegative average over the line-point distribution\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: transfers to one restricted component.  The line-point law places weight\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `1 / 2` on each of its two kinds and, inside each kind, weight `1 / m` on each\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: coordinate index, so every restricted component carries mixture weight\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `1 / (2 m)` and a nonnegative average inflates by at most `2 m`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: The estimates are items 1 and 2 of blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, formalizing the unlabelled estimates at\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1052-1058`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: /-! ## Averages of the distribution operations -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: an average against a dependent bind is the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: iterated average.  Blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1052-1058`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: an average against a convex mixture is the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: convex combination of the two averages.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1052-1058`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: an average against a product law is the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: iterated average.  Blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1056-1058`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: one component of a uniform mixture carries at\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: most the whole nonnegative average, scaled by its mixture weight.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1052-1056`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: /-! ## Inflation of a restricted line-point average -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: /-- Restricting a nonnegative line-point average to one kind and one coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: index inflates it by at most `2 m`.  This is the pointwise form of item 1 of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1052-1056`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: /-- Restricting a nonnegative average over two independent line-point samples\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: to one kind and coordinate index in each factor inflates it by at most\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `4 m ^ 2`.  This is the pointwise form of item 2 of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedAverage.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1056-1058`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: # Uniform coordinate-index mixtures of the line-point laws\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: This module proves that the axis-parallel and diagonal line-point laws are the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: uniform mixtures of their coordinate-index restrictions.  The identity rests on\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: the exact seed decomposition of `chiIndex`: the retained scalar seed is uniform,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: so each of its `m` coordinate-index fibers carries mass `1 / m`, and every\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: line conditioning map keeps that seed.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: The mixture assertion is the opening sentence of blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, formalizing the unlabelled observation at\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1049-1051`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: The restricted laws are blueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper lines\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: 1038--1048.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-! ## Elementary rewriting of finite distributions -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: the mass of a decidable event is the average\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: of its indicator.  Blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1049-1051`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: a push-forward commutes with a dependent\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: bind.  Blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1049-1051`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: a family of normalized restrictions along the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: fibers of a classifying map recovers the original law when every fiber carries\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: the uniform mass `1 / |ι|`.  This is the mixture step of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1049-1051`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-! ## The uniform coordinate-index law of the retained seed -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-- The retained scalar seed of a uniformly sampled low-degree vector is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: uniform.  This is a public copy of the private\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `MIPStarRE.QPBT.map_uniformDistribution_seed` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `MIPStarRE/QPBT/Test/LowDegreeGameTheorems.lean`, which belongs to another\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: packet; the duplication is tracked by issue #204.  Formalization-only\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: auxiliary for blueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-- The coordinate index of the retained seed of a uniformly sampled low-degree\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: vector is uniform.  Formalization-only auxiliary for blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-! ## The mass of a coordinate-index event -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-- Every coordinate-index event of a line conditioning map that keeps the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: shared scalar coordinate carries mass `1 / m`.  This is the equal-weight\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: statement behind blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1049-1051`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-! ## The two source mixture identities -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-- The axis-parallel line-point law is the uniform mixture of its\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: coordinate-index restrictions.  This is the axis half of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1049-1051`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: /-- The diagonal line-point law is the uniform mixture of its coordinate-index\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: restrictions.  This is the diagonal half of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: blueprint `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/RestrictedMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1049-1051`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: # Rewriting rules for dependent mixtures of finite distributions\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: This module records the elementary rewriting rules for the dependent mixture\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: of finite distributions that the sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: uses: a mixture indexed along a push-forward is the mixture indexed along the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: original law, iterated mixtures associate, a mixture is unchanged when the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: mixed family is changed only outside the index support, a mixture of a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: constant family is that constant, a product with a fixed independent factor\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: distributes over a mixture, and an equal two-term mixture is the mixture\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: indexed by a uniform pair of labels.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: The rewriting rules support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: The mixture, product and equal-mixture operations are those of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: blueprint `def:line-point-dist`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: /-! ## Reindexing a dependent mixture -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: /-- Mixing along a push-forward index is mixing along the original index.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: /-- A dependent mixture depends on the mixed family only on the index\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: support.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: /-- A dependent mixture of a constant family is that constant law.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: /-! ## Associativity of dependent mixtures -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: /-- Iterated dependent mixtures associate.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: /-! ## Products with an independent factor -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: /-- A product with a fixed independent second factor distributes over a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: dependent mixture of the first factor.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: /-! ## The equal two-term mixture as a uniform dependent mixture -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: /-- The equal mixture of two laws is the dependent mixture indexed by a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: uniform pair of labels.  Blueprint `def:line-point-dist`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:274-287`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBind.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: # Independent blocks of the restricted line-point laws\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: This module isolates the independent generating blocks of the restricted\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: line-point laws used by the sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma.  A\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: uniformly sampled low-degree vector splits into a point block and a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: seed-direction block; the coordinate-index event constrains only the second\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: block, so conditioning on it leaves the point block uniform and independent.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: The conditioned seed-direction block is the fresh diagonal direction pair of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: the sub-line construction, and the uniform mixture of those pairs over the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: coordinate index is the unconditioned law.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: The generating descriptions support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: The restricted laws themselves are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: 1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-! ## Uniform laws on products -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: the uniform law on a product of two finite\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: types is the product of the uniform laws.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: the mass of an event depending only on the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: second factor is the same under a uniform product law and under the uniform\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: law of that factor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-! ## Conditioning one factor of a product -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: normalized restriction depends on the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: restricted law only through its support and weights.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- Restricting a product law to an event depending on the second factor alone\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: leaves the first factor unchanged and independent of the conditioned second\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: factor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- Conditioning a uniform product law on an event of its second factor leaves\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: the first factor uniform and independent.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-! ## The block decomposition of an ambient low-degree vector -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- Split an ambient low-degree vector into its point block and its\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: seed-direction block.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:31-391`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- The coordinate-index event of `def:ith-restricted-line` read on the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: seed-direction block of an ambient low-degree vector.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- Decidability of the coordinate-index event on the seed-direction block. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- Each coordinate-index event of the seed-direction block carries mass\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `1 / m` under the uniform law, because the retained seed is uniform and its\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `m` coordinate-index fibers are equal.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1049-1051`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- Every coordinate-index event of the seed-direction block has positive\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: mass.  Blueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-! ## Fresh diagonal direction pairs -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- The fresh diagonal direction pair at coordinate index `i`: a scalar seed\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: uniform in the fiber of `i` together with a direction block uniform on the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: whole space.  This is the auxiliary randomness of the sampling procedure of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- Every fresh diagonal direction pair law has total mass one. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- The uniform mixture over the coordinate index of the fresh diagonal\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: direction pairs is the unconditioned uniform seed-direction law.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-! ## Independence of the point block under the coordinate-index event -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- Conditioning the uniform ambient low-degree law on the coordinate-index\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: event of its seed leaves the point block uniform and independent of the fresh\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: diagonal direction pair carried by the seed-direction block.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-! ## Generating form of the restricted line-point laws -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- The axis-line decoder read on the block decomposition. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- The diagonal-line decoder read on the block decomposition. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- The `i`-th restricted axis-line law is the law of the canonical\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: axis-parallel line through a uniform point block, in the coordinate direction\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: named by an independent fresh diagonal direction pair at index `i`, together\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: with that same point.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: /-- The `i`-th restricted diagonal-line law is the law of the canonical\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: diagonal line through a uniform point block, in the prefix-projected direction\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: of an independent fresh diagonal direction pair at index `i`, together with\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: that same point.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: # The branch data of the sub-line sampling procedure\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: The sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma draws a line kind, a stored\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: extended coordinate, and an unrestricted extended direction, and forms from\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: them the direction of the extended line: the elementary direction at the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: stored coordinate in the axis branch, and the extended direction with the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: coordinates preceding the stored coordinate replaced by zero in the diagonal\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: branch.  The two source lines are then read off the two coordinate blocks.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: This module fixes the coordinate index that each source line must carry: the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: stored coordinate itself when it belongs to that block, the first coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: otherwise.  With that choice the source block of the extended direction always\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: lies in the line spanned by the corresponding source direction, which is the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: incidence hypothesis of the two projection lemmas.  The module also records\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: that moving an extended point to its canonical representative does not change\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: the canonical representative of either of its source blocks.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: The statements support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: The coordinate blocks are those of `def:combine-map`, blueprint lines\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: 445--480, paper lines 970--989; the prefix truncation is that of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: blueprint `def:ld-question-distribution`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-! ## The three ranges of extended coordinates -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- Every extended coordinate is an `X`-block coordinate, a `Z`-block\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: coordinate, or one of the two scalar coordinates.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-! ## Linearity of the identification of the two scalar fields -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- The identification of the two scalar fields is additive on extended\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: points.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- The identification of the two scalar fields carries a scalar multiple of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: an extended point to the multiple by the identified scalar.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-! ## The coordinate index carried by each source line -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- The first coordinate of the source space.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `eq:chi-func`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:958-961`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- The coordinate index carried by the source `X` line of a branch: the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: stored extended coordinate when it is an `X`-block coordinate, and the first\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: coordinate otherwise.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- The coordinate index carried by the source `Z` line of a branch: the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: stored extended coordinate when it is a `Z`-block coordinate, and the first\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: coordinate otherwise.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- At an `X`-block coordinate the source `X` line carries that coordinate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- At a `Z`-block coordinate the source `X` line carries the first\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: coordinate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- At the first scalar coordinate the source `X` line carries the first\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: coordinate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- At the second scalar coordinate the source `X` line carries the first\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: coordinate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- At an `X`-block coordinate the source `Z` line carries the first\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: coordinate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- At a `Z`-block coordinate the source `Z` line carries that coordinate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- At the first scalar coordinate the source `Z` line carries the first\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: coordinate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- At the second scalar coordinate the source `Z` line carries the first\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: coordinate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-! ## The two directions of a branch -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- The direction of the extended line of a branch: the elementary direction\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: at the stored coordinate in the axis branch, and the extended direction with\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: the coordinates preceding the stored coordinate replaced by zero in the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: diagonal branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- The direction of a source line of a branch: the elementary direction at\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: its coordinate index in the axis branch, and the given source block with the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: coordinates preceding that index replaced by zero in the diagonal branch.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-! ## Incidence of the two source blocks of a branch direction -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- The `X` block of the direction of the extended line of a branch lies in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: the line spanned by the direction of its source `X` line.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- The `Z` block of the direction of the extended line of a branch lies in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: the line spanned by the direction of its source `Z` line.  In the diagonal\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: branch at an `X`-block coordinate the `Z` block is untruncated, which is why\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: the source `Z` line carries the first coordinate there.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-! ## Canonical representatives of the two source blocks -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- Replacing an extended point by its canonical representative in the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: direction of the extended line does not change the canonical representative\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: of its `X` block in the direction of the source `X` line, as soon as the `X`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: block of the extended direction lies in the line spanned by the source\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: direction.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: /-- Replacing an extended point by its canonical representative in the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: direction of the extended line does not change the canonical representative\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: of its `Z` block in the direction of the source `Z` line, as soon as the `Z`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: block of the extended direction lies in the line spanned by the source\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: direction.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: # The sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: This module carries out the sampling procedure of `lem:qld-sublines`.  A line\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: kind is drawn from the two kinds, an extended coordinate and an extended point\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: and direction are drawn uniformly, and two scalar seeds are drawn\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: independently in the coordinate-index fibers of the two coordinates that the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: two source lines must carry.  The sampled triple consists of the axis or\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: diagonal line of the extended sample together with the two source lines\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: decoded from the two coordinate blocks of the extended point and direction and\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: from the two seeds.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: The module records the elementary evaluations of that triple, the description\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: of the support of the resulting law, the three pointwise properties of a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: sampled triple -- incidence of the two source blocks, compatibility of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: affine data, and closure of the axis branch -- and the identification of its\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: extended-line marginal.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: The construction is the proof of `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: The restricted laws are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: 1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-! ## The auxiliary randomness of one branch -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- An extended point together with an extended direction.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The auxiliary randomness of one branch of the sampling procedure: an\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: extended point, an extended direction, and the two scalar seeds of the two\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: source lines.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The law of the scalar seed carried by a source line at a given coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: index: uniform on the coordinate-index fiber of that index.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- Every seed law of a source line has total mass one. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The auxiliary randomness law of the sampling procedure: the extended\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: coordinate is uniform, the extended point and direction are uniform and\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: independent of it, and the two seeds are independent and uniform on the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: coordinate-index fibers of the two coordinates carried by the two source\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: lines.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The auxiliary randomness law has total mass one. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-! ## The sampled triple -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The extended line of a branch: the canonical axis or diagonal line of an\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: extended sample.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The extended line of a branch, read off the auxiliary randomness.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- A source line of a branch: the canonical axis or diagonal line decoded\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: from a point block, a scalar seed and a direction block.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The triple sampled by one branch of the procedure: the extended line\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: together with the two source lines decoded from its two coordinate blocks and\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: from the two scalar seeds.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: paper `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The law of the triples sampled by one branch.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The sub-line law of `lem:qld-sublines`: the equal mixture of the two\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: branches of the sampling procedure.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-! ## Elementary evaluations of the sampled triple -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The extended line of a branch has the kind of that branch. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The direction of the extended line of a branch is the branch direction. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The base of the extended line of a branch is the canonical representative\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: of the drawn point in the branch direction. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- A source line of a branch has the kind of that branch. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The direction of a source line of a branch is the branch source direction\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: at the coordinate index named by its seed. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The base of a source line of a branch is the canonical representative of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: its point block in its own direction. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-! ## The support of the sub-line law -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- Every triple in the support of the sub-line law is sampled by one branch\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: from auxiliary randomness whose two seeds lie in the coordinate-index fibers\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: of the two coordinates carried by the two source lines.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-! ## Pointwise properties of a sampled triple -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The direction of the source `X` line of a sampled triple is the branch\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: source direction at the coordinate carried by that line. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The direction of the source `Z` line of a sampled triple is the branch\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: source direction at the coordinate carried by that line. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The `X` block of the direction of the extended line of a sampled triple\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: lies in the line spanned by the direction of its source `X` line. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The `Z` block of the direction of the extended line of a sampled triple\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: lies in the line spanned by the direction of its source `Z` line. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The base of the source `X` line of a sampled triple is the canonical\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: representative, in its own direction, of the `X` block of the base of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: extended line.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The base of the source `Z` line of a sampled triple is the canonical\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: representative, in its own direction, of the `Z` block of the base of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: extended line.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- Every point of the extended line of a sampled triple projects onto both of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: its source lines.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- Every sampled triple carries affine data sufficient for\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `combineLinePoly_spec`.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- An extended axis line of a sampled triple has axis-parallel source lines.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-! ## Total mass and extended marginal -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The sub-line law has total mass one. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The index-first decomposition of an extended direct sample, read at the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: bare extended dimension.  This is the decomposition\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `directLdSpaceIndexEquiv` of `def:ld-question-distribution` with its stored\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: coordinate typed by the bare dimension `2 m + 2`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The extended-line marginal of one branch of the sub-line law is the law of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: the canonical line of the corresponding kind through a uniformly sampled\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: extended direct sample.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: /-- The extended-line marginal of the sub-line law is the extended-line\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: marginal of the directly indexed line-point law.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: # Coordinate blocks of an extended point and of an extended direct sample\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: This module records the block decomposition used by the sampling procedure of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: the sub-line lemma on the extended dimension.  A point of the extended space\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: is the tuple consisting of its two source point blocks and its two scalar\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: coordinates, and this decomposition is a bijection carrying the uniform law to\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: the uniform law; in particular the two source blocks of a uniform extended\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: point are independent and uniform.  The decomposition is linear, so it\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: commutes with the affine parameterization of a line, and it commutes with the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: canonical identification of the extended scalar field with the source scalar\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: field.  The stored coordinate index of an extended direct sample splits along\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: the same three coordinate ranges.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: The decompositions support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: The coordinate blocks are those of `def:combine-map`, blueprint lines\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: 445--480, paper lines 970--989.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-! ## Linearity of the coordinate blocks -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The `X` block of a sum of extended points is the sum of the blocks.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The `Z` block of a sum of extended points is the sum of the blocks.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The `X` block of a scalar multiple of an extended point is the scalar\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: multiple of the block.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The `Z` block of a scalar multiple of an extended point is the scalar\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: multiple of the block.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-! ## The coordinate blocks of an extended point -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- Split an extended point into its two source point blocks and its two\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: scalar coordinates.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The first block of the decomposition is the `X` block. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The second block of the decomposition is the `Z` block. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The first scalar coordinate of the decomposition is `alpha`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The second scalar coordinate of the decomposition is `beta`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The block decomposition of an extended point carries the uniform law to\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: the uniform law.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The two source blocks of a uniformly sampled extended point are\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: independent and uniform.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-! ## Transport to the source scalar field -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The `X` block of an extended point transported to the source scalar field\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: is the transport of its `X` block.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The `Z` block of an extended point transported to the source scalar field\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: is the transport of its `Z` block.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-! ## The blocks of an extended direct sample -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- The stored index, point block and direction block of a uniformly sampled\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: direct low-degree vector are independent and uniform.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-! ## Compatibility data read off the coordinate blocks -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- An extended line is compatible with two source lines as soon as each of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: its two point blocks is an affine reparameterization of the corresponding\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: source line: the base block is a point of the source line and the direction\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: block is a multiple of the source direction.  The two scalar coordinates of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: the compatibility data are the `alpha` and `beta` coordinates of the extended\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: base and direction.  This supplies the domain condition preceding Equation\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `eq:combine-lines` in blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: /-- An extended line whose two source lines carry the inherited direction\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: blocks is compatible with them, with both affine reparameterizations the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: identity.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: # The projected mixtures of the sub-line law\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: This module assembles the two projected point marginals of the sub-line law of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `lem:qld-sublines` into mixtures of the restricted product laws.  Drawing a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: uniform point of the extended line of a mixture of line laws is the same\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: mixture of the corresponding uniform-point laws, so the two projected\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: marginals of the sub-line law are the mixtures, over the drawn kind and the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: drawn extended coordinate, of the projected marginals of one branch at one\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: extended coordinate.  Each of those is the component law of the pair of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: indices carried by that branch, so the mixing law is the law of the kind\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: together with the pair of indices assigned to a uniform extended coordinate.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: The assembly is the last step of the proof of `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: The restricted laws are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: 1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-! ## The uniform point of a mixture of line laws -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- Drawing a uniform point of the extended line of a mixture of line-triple\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: laws is the corresponding mixture of the uniform-point laws.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-! ## The projected marginals of one branch at one extended coordinate -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- One branch of the sampling procedure, read at a fixed extended coordinate,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: is the law of the triple decoded from the auxiliary randomness of that\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: coordinate.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- The `X` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch at\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: one extended coordinate is the `X` component law of the pair of indices of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: that branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- The `Z` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch at\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: one extended coordinate is the `Z` component law of the pair of indices of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: that branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-! ## The mixing law of the two indices -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- The law of the kind and the pair of source indices assigned to a uniform\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: extended coordinate of one branch.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- The mixing law of the sub-line lemma: the equal mixture over the two kinds\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: of the law of the pair of source indices of a uniform extended coordinate.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- The mixing law has total mass one. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-! ## The two projected marginals of one branch -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- The `X` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: the mixture, along the pair of source indices of a uniform extended\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: coordinate, of the `X` component laws.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- The `Z` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: the mixture, along the pair of source indices of a uniform extended\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: coordinate, of the `Z` component laws.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-! ## The two projected marginals of the sub-line law -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- The `X` point marginal of the sub-line law is the mixture of the `X`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: component laws along the mixing law.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- The `Z` point marginal of the sub-line law is the mixture of the `Z`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: component laws along the mixing law.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: /-- The two one-point projected marginals of the sub-line law are mixtures of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: the restricted product laws along one common mixing law.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: # Coordinate blocks of a truncated extended direction\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: This module computes the two source blocks of the direction of an extended\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: diagonal line.  That direction is obtained from an unrestricted extended\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: vector by zeroing the coordinates that precede the stored coordinate index,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: so each source block is again a prefix truncation: when the stored index is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: the `i`-th coordinate of a block, that block inherits the truncation at `i`,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: preceding blocks vanish, and later blocks remain untruncated.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: The computation is recorded after transport to the source scalar field, since\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: the extended direction and the source line directions are presented over the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: two identified scalar fields.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: The block computations support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: The truncation is the prefix restriction of blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `def:ld-question-distribution`; the coordinate blocks are those of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: blueprint `def:combine-map`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-! ## Truncation at the first coordinate -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-- Zeroing the coordinates preceding the first coordinate changes nothing.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: Blueprint `def:ld-question-distribution`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:31-391`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-! ## The `X` block of a truncated extended direction -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at an `X`-block\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: coordinate is the `X` block truncated at that coordinate of the source space.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at a `Z`-block\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: coordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at the first scalar\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: coordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at the second scalar\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: coordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-! ## The `Z` block of a truncated extended direction -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at a `Z`-block\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: coordinate is the `Z` block truncated at that coordinate of the source space.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at an `X`-block\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: coordinate is the untruncated `Z` block, since every `Z`-block coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: follows every `X`-block coordinate.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at the first scalar\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: coordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: /-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at the second scalar\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: coordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: # Products and push-forwards of finite distributions\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: The sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma presents one law both as an\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: independent product of its generating blocks and as a dependent mixture over\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: one of those blocks.  This module records the three rewriting rules that pass\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: between the two presentations: a push-forward of the first factor of a product\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: is a push-forward of the product, a product with a probability law in the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: second factor has the first factor as its marginal, and a push-forward of a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: product is the dependent mixture of the push-forwards of its second factor.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: The rewriting rules support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: The product and mixture operations are those of blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: `def:line-point-dist`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: /-! ## Nonemptiness of the support of a probability law -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: /-- A law of total mass one has a nonempty support.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: /-! ## Push-forward of one factor of a product -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: /-- Pushing the first factor of a product forward is pushing the product\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: forward along the map acting on that factor alone.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: /-! ## The first marginal of a product -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: /-- The first marginal of a product whose second factor is a probability law\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: is the first factor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: /-- Reading a function of the first factor of a product whose second factor is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: a probability law is reading it on the first factor.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: /-! ## A product as a dependent mixture -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: /-- A push-forward of a product is the dependent mixture, over the first\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: factor, of the push-forwards of the second factor.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: # The two independent blocks of a fresh diagonal direction pair\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: This module splits the fresh diagonal direction pair of the sub-line sampling\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: procedure into its two independent blocks.  The coordinate-index event\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: constrains only the scalar seed of the pair, so conditioning on it leaves the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: direction block uniform and independent of the conditioned seed.  The\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: direction block of a fresh pair is therefore available as an unconstrained\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: uniform vector, which is what allows the sampling procedure to read it off the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: corresponding block of the direction of the sampled extended line.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: The block decomposition supports `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: The coordinate-index event is that of `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: lines 1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-! ## Conditioning the first factor of a product -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-- Restricting a product law to an event depending on the first factor alone\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: leaves the second factor unchanged and independent of the conditioned first\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: factor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: the mass of an event depending only on the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: first factor is the same under a uniform product law and under the uniform\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: law of that factor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-- Conditioning a uniform product law on an event of its first factor leaves\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: the second factor uniform and independent.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-! ## The coordinate-index event on a scalar seed -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-- The coordinate-index event of `def:ith-restricted-line` read on a scalar\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: seed alone.  Blueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-- Decidability of the coordinate-index event on a scalar seed. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-- Every coordinate-index event of a scalar seed has positive mass under the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: uniform law.  Blueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-! ## The blocks of a fresh diagonal direction pair -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-- A fresh diagonal direction pair at coordinate index `i` is a scalar seed\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: conditioned on the coordinate-index fiber of `i` together with an independent\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: uniform direction block.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: /-- The direction block of a fresh diagonal direction pair is uniform.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: # Reshuffling products of finitely supported laws\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: The sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma produces its six independent\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: generating blocks in one grouping, while the restricted line-point laws that\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: the projected marginals must match present the same six blocks in another\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: grouping.  This module records the rewriting rules that pass between two such\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: presentations: the support and the weight of a product, the fact that a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: push-forward along a bijection is determined by the supports and weights it\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: compares, and the push-forward of the second factor of a product.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: The rewriting rules support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: The product operation is that of blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: `def:line-point-dist`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: /-! ## The support and the weight of a product -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: /-- The support of a product law is the product of the supports.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: `def:line-point-dist`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:274-287`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: /-- The weight of a product law is the product of the weights.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: `def:line-point-dist`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:274-287`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: /-! ## Push-forward along a bijection -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: /-- A push-forward along a bijection is identified by the supports and the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: weights it compares: if the map matches the two supports and carries each\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: weight to the corresponding weight, and if it has a two-sided inverse, the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: push-forward is the target law.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: /-! ## Push-forward of the second factor of a product -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: /-- Pushing the second factor of a product forward is pushing the product\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: forward along the map acting on that factor alone.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: # The projected marginals of the sub-line law\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: This module identifies the two projected point marginals of the sub-line law\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: of `lem:qld-sublines` as mixtures of the restricted product laws.  The two\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: source lines of a sampled triple depend on the drawn extended point only\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: through the canonical representatives of its two source blocks, and those\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: representatives are unchanged when the drawn point is replaced by any point of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: the extended line; a uniform point of the extended line through a uniform\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: point is a uniform point, so the drawn point may be replaced throughout by the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: point that is finally retained.  The six generating blocks of the resulting\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: law -- the two source point blocks, the two scalar seeds and the two source\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: direction blocks -- are then regrouped into the two restricted product laws\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: carried by the two indices of the branch.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: The identification is the last step of the proof of `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: The restricted laws are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: 1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-! ## Source lines depend on the point block only through its representative -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-- Two source lines of the same branch with the same seed and the same source\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: direction block agree as soon as their point blocks have the same canonical\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: representative in the direction of that line.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-- Replacing the drawn extended point by a point of the extended line does\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: not change the canonical representative of its `X` block in the direction of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: the source `X` line of the branch.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-- Replacing the drawn extended point by a point of the extended line does\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: not change the canonical representative of its `Z` block in the direction of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: the source `Z` line of the branch.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-! ## Generating form of the restricted product components -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-- The restricted line-point law of a kind at an index is the law of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: source line of that kind decoded from a uniform point block, a scalar seed in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: the coordinate-index fiber of the index and a uniform direction block,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: together with that same point block.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-- The `X` component law of a pair of indices is the law of the two source\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: lines decoded from two independent restricted blocks, together with the point\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: block of the first.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-- The `Z` component law of a pair of indices is the law of the two source\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: lines decoded from two independent restricted blocks, together with the point\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: block of the second.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-! ## The joint projected marginal of one branch at one extended coordinate -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-- At a fixed line kind and a fixed extended coordinate, the joint law of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: two source lines of a sampled triple together with the two source blocks of a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: uniform point of its extended line is the law of the two source lines decoded\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: from two independent restricted blocks together with their two point blocks.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-! ## The two one-point projected marginals of one branch -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-- At a fixed line kind and a fixed extended coordinate, the joint law of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: two source lines of a sampled triple together with the source `X` block of a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: uniform point of its extended line is the `X` component law of the two indices\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: of that branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: /-- At a fixed line kind and a fixed extended coordinate, the joint law of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: two source lines of a sampled triple together with the source `Z` block of a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: uniform point of its extended line is the `Z` component law of the two indices\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: of that branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: # Generating laws of the restricted line-point distributions\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: This module rewrites the two restricted line-point laws of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `def:ith-restricted-line` as push-forwards of a single uniformly sampled\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: low-degree vector conditioned on the coordinate-index fiber of its retained\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: scalar seed.  In that form the point block, the seed and the direction block\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: of the sampled vector are visibly the independent generating data of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: restricted law, which is the description used by the sampling procedure of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: sub-line lemma: there the projected pairs are built either from a direction\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: block inherited from the extended line or from freshly sampled data, and each\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: is asserted to follow a restricted law.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: The generating descriptions support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: The restricted laws themselves are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: 1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-! ## Restriction of a push-forward -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-- Formalization-only auxiliary: restricting a push-forward distribution to a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: decidable event is the push-forward of the restriction to the pre-image of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: that event.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-! ## The coordinate-index event on a single uniform vector -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-- The coordinate-index event of `def:ith-restricted-line` read on the single\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: uniformly sampled low-degree vector that generates the line-point laws.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: Blueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-- Decidability of the coordinate-index event. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-- The coordinate-index event has positive mass under the uniform law on\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: low-degree vectors, so the conditioning below is normalized.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-! ## Canonical representatives are unchanged by the conditioning maps -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-- Two axis-line descriptions with the same seed and the same base agree:\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: the stored proof of `def:line-representative` invariance is a proposition and\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: carries no further data.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-- Two diagonal-line descriptions with the same seed, the same base and the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: same direction agree: the stored proofs of `def:line-representative`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: invariance and of the prefix-zero condition are propositions and carry no\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: further data.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-- The axis-line decoder is unchanged by the axis conditioning map: the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: canonical representative of `def:line-representative` is idempotent, so\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: decoding the conditioned vector and decoding the original vector agree.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-- The diagonal-line decoder is unchanged by the diagonal conditioning map:\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: the prefix projection and the canonical representative of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `def:line-representative` are both idempotent.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-! ## Generating laws of the restricted line-point distributions -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-- The `i`-th restricted axis-line law is the law of the pair consisting of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: the canonical axis-parallel line through a low-degree vector and its point\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: block, when that vector is uniform conditioned on the coordinate-index fiber\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: of its seed.  This is the generating description used in the sampling\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: procedure of blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: /-- The `i`-th restricted diagonal-line law is the law of the pair consisting\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: of the canonical diagonal line through a low-degree vector and its point\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: block, when that vector is uniform conditioned on the coordinate-index fiber\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: of its seed.  This is the generating description used in the sampling\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: procedure of blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: # Uniformity under the identification of the two scalar fields\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: The extended line of the sub-line construction lives over the scalar field of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: the directly indexed extended game, while the two source lines live over the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: scalar field of the Pauli basis test.  The two fields are identified\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: coordinatewise by a field isomorphism, so that identification is a bijection\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: of the coordinate spaces and therefore carries uniform laws to uniform laws.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: This module records that transport for a whole extended point and for the two\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: source blocks of an extended point, and it records the resampling rule that\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: reading a function at the point of a uniformly random affine parameter on the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: line through the canonical representative of a uniformly random point is the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: same as reading that function at a uniformly random point.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: The statements support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: The coordinate blocks are those of `def:combine-map`, blueprint lines\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: 445--480, paper lines 970--989.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-! ## The identification of the two scalar fields as a bijection -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-- The coordinatewise identification of the extended scalar field with the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: source scalar field on one source block, presented as a bijection.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-- The coordinatewise identification of the extended scalar field with the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: source scalar field on a whole extended point, presented as a bijection.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-- The `X` block of a transported extended point is the transport of its `X`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: block.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-- The `Z` block of a transported extended point is the transport of its `Z`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: block.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-! ## Transport of the uniform laws -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-- A uniformly random extended point transported to the source scalar field\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: is uniformly random.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-- A uniformly random source block transported to the source scalar field is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: uniformly random.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-- The two source blocks of a uniformly random extended point, read in the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: source scalar field, are independent and uniformly random.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-! ## Resampling a uniform point along a line -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: /-- Reading a function at the point of a uniformly random affine parameter on\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: the line through the canonical representative, in a fixed direction, of a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: uniformly random point is the same as reading that function at a uniformly\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: random point.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: # Uniform points of a line and the blocks of a coordinate direction\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: This module records the two elementary geometric inputs of the sampling\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: procedure of the sub-line lemma. First, for independent uniform inputs `y` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: the ambient coordinate space and `t` in the scalar field, the point\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `lineRepMap v y + t • v` is uniform. This map on ambient-point--parameter pairs\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: has fibers of size `q`: for nonzero `v`, `y` varies over a coset of the span of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `v`, while its canonical representative and `t` are fixed; for zero `v`, `y`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: is fixed and `t` is free. Second, the two coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: blocks of an elementary coordinate direction of the extended space are the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: corresponding elementary direction of the source space in the block that\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: carries the coordinate, and zero in the other block and at the two scalar\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: coordinates.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: The two statements support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: The canonical representative map is blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `def:line-representative`; the coordinate blocks are those of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: blueprint `def:combine-map`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-! ## The canonical representative for the zero direction -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- For the zero direction the canonical representative map of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `def:line-representative` is the identity, since the span of the zero vector\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: is trivial.  Blueprint `def:line-representative`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:143-174`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-! ## Uniform points of a line -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The point at a uniformly random affine parameter on the line through the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: canonical representative of a uniformly random point is uniformly random on\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: the whole coordinate space.  Every fiber of the parameterization has exactly\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `q` elements in the ambient-point--parameter domain: for a nonzero direction\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: the parameter and canonical representative are determined, while the input\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: point varies over a coset of the span of the direction. For the zero direction\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: the input point is fixed and the parameter is free. Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-! ## Injectivity of the two block embeddings -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `X`-block coordinate embedding of `def:combine-map` is injective.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `Z`-block coordinate embedding of `def:combine-map` is injective.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-! ## The blocks of an elementary coordinate direction -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `X` block of the elementary direction at an `X`-block coordinate is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: the elementary direction of the source space at that coordinate.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `X` block of the elementary direction at a `Z`-block coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: vanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `X` block of the elementary direction at the first scalar coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: vanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `X` block of the elementary direction at the second scalar coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: vanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `Z` block of the elementary direction at a `Z`-block coordinate is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: the elementary direction of the source space at that coordinate.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `Z` block of the elementary direction at an `X`-block coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: vanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `Z` block of the elementary direction at the first scalar coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: vanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `Z` block of the elementary direction at the second scalar coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: vanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-! ## The coordinates occupied by the two blocks -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `i`-th `X`-block coordinate of the extended space is its `i`-th\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: coordinate.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `i`-th `Z`-block coordinate of the extended space is its `(m + i)`-th\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: coordinate.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The extended coordinate carrying the first scalar is the coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `2 m`.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The extended coordinate carrying the second scalar is the coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `2 m + 1`.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-! ## The blocks of a point of an extended line -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- Transporting the extended scalar field to the source scalar field carries\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: a point of a line to a point of the transported line, since the transport is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: a coordinatewise field isomorphism.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `X` block of a point of a line lies on the source line through the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: canonical representative of the `X` block of the base, as soon as the `X`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: block of the direction is a multiple of the source direction.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The `Z` block of a point of a line lies on the source line through the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: canonical representative of the `Z` block of the base, as soon as the `Z`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: block of the direction is a multiple of the source direction.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-! ## Incidence of the blocks of an extended line -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- The identification of the extended scalar field with the source scalar\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: field carries an elementary coordinate direction to the elementary coordinate\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: direction at the same coordinate.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- Incidence of the `X` blocks: if a source line has as base the canonical\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: representative, in its own direction, of the `X` block of the base of an\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: extended line, and if the `X` block of the direction of the extended line\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: lies in the span of the source direction, then the `X` block of every point\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: of the extended line is a point of the source line.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: /-- Incidence of the `Z` blocks: if a source line has as base the canonical\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: representative, in its own direction, of the `Z` block of the base of an\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: extended line, and if the `Z` block of the direction of the extended line\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: lies in the span of the source direction, then the `Z` block of every point\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: of the extended line is a point of the source line.  Blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: # The ordered point product on a single placement\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: The consistency guarantees carried by the joint point measurement compare it\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: with the ordered `Z`-then-`X` product across a pair of opposite placements.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: The scalar estimates used to combine the two Pauli bases instead need the two\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: families on one placement.  This module obtains that form by inserting the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: joint measurement on the opposite placement and applying the squared-distance\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: triangle inequality twice.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: The statement is `lem:qld-4-10-same-placement` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: `blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex`; it restates the ordered\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: `Z`-then-`X` display of `lem:qld-4-10`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:689-709`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: /-- Every placement has an opposite placement, and the relation is symmetric\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: between the two members of each pair. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: /-- On a single placement, the joint point measurement is close to the ordered\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OrderedPoints.lean: `Z`-then-`X` point product, with inflation factor four. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: # Replacing one factor of a measurement-weighted overlap\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: The scalar estimates used to combine the two Pauli bases compare averaged\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: overlaps whose left factor is one complete measurement and whose right factors\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: are operator families at small state-dependent distance.  This module records\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: the corresponding Cauchy--Schwarz estimate.  The right-hand families are\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: arbitrary, so the estimate covers an ordered product of point effects, which\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: is not itself a measurement.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: The estimate is `lem:overlap-gap-distance` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: `blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex`; it is the Cauchy--Schwarz\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: step at paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1147-1166`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- Replacing one operator family inside an overlap weighted on the left by a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: complete measurement changes the average by at most the square root of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: state-dependent squared distance between the two families. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-! ## The Cauchy--Schwarz inequality weighted by a positive operator -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- For a positive operator `A`, the sesquilinear form `⟨u, A v⟩` satisfies the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: Cauchy--Schwarz inequality with the two diagonal values `⟨u, A u⟩` and\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: `⟨v, A v⟩`.  This is the inequality behind the Cauchy--Schwarz steps of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: proofs of `lem:claim-17-2` and `lem:claim-17-3`, in which both factors carry\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: the square root of the line effect, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1168-1209`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- The sum over outcomes of the real parts of `⟨u a, A a (v a)⟩`, for\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: positive weights `A a`, is bounded by the product of the square roots of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: two diagonal sums. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- The averaged form of the preceding estimate: the average over a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: distribution of the outcome sums of `Re ⟨u, A v⟩` is bounded by the product of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: the square roots of the two averaged diagonal sums. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-! ## Quadratic forms against projections -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- The real quadratic form is invariant under the conjugate transpose. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- Conjugating an operator transfers the outer operator to the state vector. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- For a projection `B` commuting with `A`, the quadratic form of `A` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: `B ψ` is the quadratic form of `A B` in `ψ`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- The quadratic form of an operator bounded by the identity is bounded by\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: the squared norm of the vector. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- The effects of a complete measurement have total quadratic form one on a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: unit vector. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- Weighting a complete measurement by commuting projections cannot exceed\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: the total mass one. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-! ## Replacing a factor under the weight of a measurement -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- Cauchy--Schwarz with the measurement as weight: for projections `B`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: commuting with the effects of `A`, the averaged overlap of `A` against the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: product `B C` is bounded by the square roots of the averaged overlap of `A`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: against `B` and of the averaged quadratic form of `A` in `C ψ`.  This is the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: Cauchy--Schwarz step of the proofs of `lem:claim-17-2` and `lem:claim-17-3`,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: paper `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1168-1209`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- Deficit form of the Cauchy--Schwarz estimate: for projections `B` and `C`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: commuting with the effects of `A`, replacing the factor `C B` by `C` inside an\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: overlap weighted by the complete measurement `A` changes the average by at\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: most the square root of the deficit `1 - E Σ_a ⟨A_a B_a⟩`.  This is the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: estimate used in the proof of `lem:claim-17-2` and, with `C = Id`, in the proof\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: of `lem:claim-17-3`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1168-1209`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- The deficit form of the Cauchy--Schwarz estimate with the product family\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: `C B` supplied as one family `CB`, which is convenient when the product is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: formed on a local space before being placed. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- Inserting an arbitrary operator family `W` after a projection family `R`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: commuting with the effects of `A` costs at most the square root of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: averaged squared norms of `W ψ`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: /-- The mirror image of the preceding estimate: an operator family `W`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: commuting with the effects of `A`, inserted before a commuting projection\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: family `R`, costs at most the square root of the averaged squared norms of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/OverlapGap.lean: `Wᴴ ψ`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: # Placement calculus on the expanded state\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: This module records the algebra of the four register placements used by the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: combining argument: a placement is a unital `*`-homomorphism, placements on\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: opposite register pairs commute, and the crosswise exchange of the two EPR\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: pairs of the expanded state carries the placement `AA'` to `AB''` and `BA''`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: to `BB'` while fixing the expanded state.  The last fact transfers every\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: state-dependent distance between the two bipartition schemes of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: `def:symmetric-equivalents` without assuming a symmetric strategy.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: The placements are those of `def:symmetric-equivalents`, blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: `blueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:1003-1030`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:420-450`.  The\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: EPR-exchange invariance is the element `U_σ U_θ` of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: `lem:symmetric-equivalents-transfer`, blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: `blueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:1032-1130`, which needs no\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: symmetry of the strategy; its use for `lem:qld-4-10` is analyzed in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: `docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_linearity-theorem-quotation.tex`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-! ## Placements as reindexed tensor factors -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- The `AA'` placement is the reindexed left factor of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: `AA' | BA''(B'B'')` bipartition. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- The `BA''` placement is the reindexed right factor of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: `AA' | BA''(B'B'')` bipartition. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- The `AB''` placement is the reindexed left factor of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: `AB'' | BB'(A'A'')` bipartition. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- The `BB'` placement is the reindexed right factor of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: `AB'' | BB'(A'A'')` bipartition. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- Reindexing an operator commutes with the conjugate transpose. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- Reindexing an operator along an equivalence preserves positivity. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-! ## The placement is a unital `*`-homomorphism -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- A placement is multiplicative. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- A placement sends the local identity to the identity of the six-register\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: space. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- A placement is additive over finite sums of local operators. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- A placement commutes with the conjugate transpose. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- A placement preserves positivity. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- The placement of a local operator bounded by the identity is bounded by\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: the identity. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- The placement of a projector is a contraction. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- Operators placed on opposite register pairs commute. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- A complete measurement on a local expanded space, placed on one of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: four register pairs, is a complete measurement on the six-register space. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- The effects of a placed measurement are the placed effects. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- A placed projective measurement is projective. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- A placement maps the zero operator to zero. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- The completed expanded point measurement agrees with the original effect\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: at a defined outcome. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Points/PlacementSupport.lean: /-- The completed expanded point measurement has zero effect at `none`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: # Sub-line averages of the Z-point overlap\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: The overlap of the paired-line measurement with the expanded `Z`-point effect\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: depends only on the two source lines and the `Z`-point. The separate Z marginal\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: of the auxiliary `SubLineWitness` law is a mixture of products of restricted\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: line-point laws, with the fresh X point unused. Summing line answers over the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: evaluation fibers at that fresh point identifies the overlap with the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: evaluated pair-line overlap, so the Z deficit estimates of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: `MIPStarRE.QPBT.Combining.ZEvalDeficit` apply component by component.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: The `_at` estimates and `zPointOverlapAt` retain arbitrary\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: opposite placements. The original overlap definitions and first-player\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: statements are unchanged. Only the separate Z marginal mixture law is used.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: Transport of the auxiliary extended-line law to the source carrier remains\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: open; see `docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility.tex`, section\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: \\\"Scalar estimates on the auxiliary subline law\\\".\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: The statements support the auxiliary `lem:claim-17-3-direct-real`, near\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: the source `lem:claim-17-3` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: `blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1204-1239`; the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: mixture is Property~2 of `lem:qld-sublines`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-! ## Regrouping the line answers by their evaluations -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- Summing a paired-line answer against a family of point effects that\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: depends on the answer only through its two evaluations is summing the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: evaluation classes of the paired-line measurement against that family.  This\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: is the fiber regrouping of the proofs of `lem:claim-17-1`, `lem:claim-17-2`,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: and `lem:claim-17-3` in the blueprint. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- The sub-line average of a function of the two source lines and the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: `Z`-point of the sampled extended point is a mixture of averages over products\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: of two restricted line-point laws, the `Z`-point being the point of the second\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: factor.  This is the use of Property~2 of `lem:qld-sublines` in the proof of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: `lem:claim-17-3`, blueprint `blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex`,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: paper `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1204-1239`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-! ## The two one-point overlaps -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- The overlap of the paired-line measurement at two source lines with the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: expanded `Z`-point effect at a point, summed over the line answers.  This is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: the integrand of the left-hand side of `lem:claim-17-3`, as a function of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: two source lines and the `Z`-point alone. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- The original first-player specialization of the placed overlap. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- At a pair of line-point pairs, the `Z`-point overlap is the overlap of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: evaluated pair-line measurement with the completed `Z`-point measurement; the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: `X`-point of the first pair only labels the evaluation classes. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- The original first-player specialization of the placed estimate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- The `Z`-point overlap is nonnegative. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- The original first-player specialization of the placed estimate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- The sub-line average of the `Z`-point overlap is at most one. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- The original first-player specialization of the placed estimate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- The deficit of the sub-line average of the `Z`-point overlap is bounded by\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: the inflated line and point consistency errors. This is the directly indexed\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: auxiliary deficit estimate supporting `lem:claim-17-3-direct-real`, near\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: the source `lem:claim-17-3`, blueprint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: `blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1204-1239`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/SubLineZDeficit.lean: /-- The original first-player specialization of the placed estimate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: # A uniform point of the extended space from the directly indexed sub-line law\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: The first scalar estimate in the combining argument samples an extended line\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: from the auxiliary `SubLineWitness` and then a uniform affine parameter on that line.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: This module records that the resulting extended point is uniform and hence\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: that its two source coordinate blocks are independent uniform points. These\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: identities concern `directLinePointDist`. Transport to the source's seed-indexed\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: carrier remains open, as recorded in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: `docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility.tex`, section\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: \\\"Scalar estimates on the auxiliary subline law\\\".\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: These statements support `lem:claim-17-1` in\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: `blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex`.  The paper uses this sampling\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: fact at\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1159-1166`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: /-- A dependent mixture of a constant family is the constant law. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: /-- If every slice of a map of a pair carries the second uniform law to one\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: fixed law, then the map carries the uniform law of the pair to that law. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: /-- Apply the shared uniform-point identity to an ambient point and an\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: independent affine parameter. This preserves the existing public name for the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: identity supplied by `uniformDistribution_map_lineRepMap_add_smul`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: /-- Reading a point at a uniform affine parameter on the canonical line of a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: uniform direct sample gives a uniform point of the direct coordinate space. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: /-- Averaging a function at a uniform affine parameter on the canonical line\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: of a uniform direct sample is averaging it at a uniform point. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: /-- A uniform point on a line drawn from the directly indexed line-point law\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: is uniform in the ambient coordinate space. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: /-- Identify the directly indexed extended coordinates with the combining\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: coordinate split. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: /-- Split an extended point into its two source blocks and two remaining\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: scalar coordinates. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: /-- The two source blocks of a uniform extended point are independent and\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: uniform after the canonical scalar identification. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: /-- Under a sub-line witness, the two source blocks of the point at a uniform\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/UniformLinePoint.lean: affine parameter are independent uniform source points. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-!\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: # Z-point deficits of the evaluated pair-line measurement\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: The proof of `lem:claim-17-3` bounds, after its Cauchy--Schwarz step, the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: deficit of the overlap between the evaluated pair-line measurement and the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: expanded `Z`-point measurement at the `Z`-point of the sample.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: This module derives that deficit from the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: consistency of the evaluated pair-line measurement with the completed joint\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: point measurement and from the closeness of the joint point measurement to the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: ordered point products. The derivation inserts the joint point measurement\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: on the point side, replaces it by the ordered product whose trailing factor is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: the retained point effect, and sums the other factor, at the cost of three\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: square roots. The deficit is first bounded over an arbitrary probability\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: law on pairs of line-point pairs and then over a product of two restricted\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: line-point laws, whose point marginal is the uniform pair.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: The `_at` variants retain arbitrary opposite placements, including Bob at\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: `BBprime` and Alice at `ABdoubleprime`. The original statements are unchanged\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: specializations; register commutation does not assume symmetry of the state.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: ## References\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: The Z deficit is the quantity bounded in the proof of `lem:claim-17-3`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: in `blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1204-1239`; the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: consistency input is `lem:qld-xz-lines` and the ordered products are those of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: `lem:qld-4-10`.\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-! ## Completed point pairs -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- A sum over completed outcome pairs of a function vanishing on both `none`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: classes is the sum over the uncompleted pairs. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- The completed joint measurement has the joint effect on a pair of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: evaluations. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- The completed joint measurement vanishes on outcomes outside the image of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: the completion. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- The completion of a projective joint measurement is projective. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- A placed projection is a projection. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- Every effect of the completed expanded point measurement is a projection,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: the `none` effect being zero. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- The completed joint point measurement and a completed point product differ\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: only on outcome pairs with two evaluations, so their placed state-dependent\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: squared distance is the one of the joint point measurement and the point\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: product.  Blueprint `ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:2651-2698`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- The completed `Z`-then-`X` point product vanishes on both `none` classes\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: and restricts to the ordered product on evaluated pairs. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-! ## The abstract one-point deficit -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- Let `A` be a complete measurement, `Q` a projective measurement, `R` a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: family of projections and `W` a family of operators, all indexed by the same\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: outcomes, with `Q`, `R` and `W` commuting with the effects of `A` and with\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: `W R = W`.  If `A` and `Q` are consistent with defect at most `η` and `Q` is\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: within squared distance `ζ` of `Wᴴ`, then the deficit of the overlap of `A`\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: with `R` is at most `2 √η + 2 √ζ`.  The proof inserts `Q` before `R`, replaces\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: `Q R` by `W R = W`, and compares `W` with `Q`; each of the three steps is a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: Cauchy--Schwarz estimate weighted by `A`.  This is the common estimate behind\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: the deficits in the proofs of `lem:claim-17-2` and `lem:claim-17-3`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1168-1239`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-! ## The two placed deficits -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- A pair of line-point pairs of the source space: the sample type of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: consistency display of `lem:qld-xz-lines`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- The `Z`-point deficit of the evaluated pair-line measurement over a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: probability law on pairs of line-point pairs, from the consistency of the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: evaluated pair-line measurement with the completed joint point measurement\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: and the closeness of the latter to the `Z`-then-`X` product.  This is the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: deficit bounded in the proof of `lem:claim-17-3`, paper\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1204-1239`. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- The original first-player specialization of the opposite-placement estimate. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-! ## The two deficits over a product of restricted line-point laws -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- Every restricted line-point law has total mass one. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- The point marginal of a restricted line-point law is uniform. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- The pair of points of a product of two restricted line-point laws is a\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: uniform pair. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- A state-dependent distance between two families depending on the pair of\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: points only, averaged over a product of two restricted line-point laws, is the\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: distance averaged over a uniform pair of points. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- The `Z`-point deficit over a product of two restricted line-point laws,\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: with the inflation `4 m ^ 2` of the line consistency error and the inflation\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: `4` of the point consistency error. -/\\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean: /-- The original first-player specialization of the opposite-placement estimate. -/\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/drx/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr400/79caf482f3bb07bf4392bccacf0f2384adf8fb8f/prose-last-message.md.dispatch.log:73:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_36\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"awk 'NR >= 1801 && NR < 3975 { if (/\\\"'^diff --git/) print; if (/''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\+[\\\"'^+]/) {s=substr($0,2); if(s ~ /''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\/-/) comment=1; if(comment) print s; if(s ~ /-\\\\\\\\//) comment=0} }' /home/drx/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr400/79caf482f3bb07bf4392bccacf0f2384adf8fb8f/diff.patch\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"diff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBlocks.lean\\n/-!\\n# Independent blocks of the restricted line-point laws\\nThis module isolates the independent generating blocks of the restricted\\nline-point laws used by the sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma.  A\\nuniformly sampled low-degree vector splits into a point block and a\\nseed-direction block; the coordinate-index event constrains only the second\\nblock, so conditioning on it leaves the point block uniform and independent.\\nThe conditioned seed-direction block is the fresh diagonal direction pair of\\nthe sub-line construction, and the uniform mixture of those pairs over the\\ncoordinate index is the unconditioned law.\\n## References\\nThe generating descriptions support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe restricted laws themselves are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\n1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Uniform laws on products -/\\n/-- Formalization-only auxiliary: the uniform law on a product of two finite\\ntypes is the product of the uniform laws.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Formalization-only auxiliary: the mass of an event depending only on the\\nsecond factor is the same under a uniform product law and under the uniform\\nlaw of that factor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Conditioning one factor of a product -/\\n/-- Formalization-only auxiliary: normalized restriction depends on the\\nrestricted law only through its support and weights.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Restricting a product law to an event depending on the second factor alone\\nleaves the first factor unchanged and independent of the conditioned second\\nfactor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Conditioning a uniform product law on an event of its second factor leaves\\nthe first factor uniform and independent.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The block decomposition of an ambient low-degree vector -/\\n/-- Split an ambient low-degree vector into its point block and its\\nseed-direction block.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:31-391`. -/\\n/-- The coordinate-index event of `def:ith-restricted-line` read on the\\nseed-direction block of an ambient low-degree vector.  Blueprint\\n`def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\n/-- Decidability of the coordinate-index event on the seed-direction block. -/\\n/-- Each coordinate-index event of the seed-direction block carries mass\\n`1 / m` under the uniform law, because the retained seed is uniform and its\\n`m` coordinate-index fibers are equal.  Blueprint\\n`lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1049-1051`. -/\\n/-- Every coordinate-index event of the seed-direction block has positive\\nmass.  Blueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\n/-! ## Fresh diagonal direction pairs -/\\n/-- The fresh diagonal direction pair at coordinate index `i`: a scalar seed\\nuniform in the fiber of `i` together with a direction block uniform on the\\nwhole space.  This is the auxiliary randomness of the sampling procedure of\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Every fresh diagonal direction pair law has total mass one. -/\\n/-- The uniform mixture over the coordinate index of the fresh diagonal\\ndirection pairs is the unconditioned uniform seed-direction law.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Independence of the point block under the coordinate-index event -/\\n/-- Conditioning the uniform ambient low-degree law on the coordinate-index\\nevent of its seed leaves the point block uniform and independent of the fresh\\ndiagonal direction pair carried by the seed-direction block.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Generating form of the restricted line-point laws -/\\n/-- The axis-line decoder read on the block decomposition. -/\\n/-- The diagonal-line decoder read on the block decomposition. -/\\n/-- The `i`-th restricted axis-line law is the law of the canonical\\naxis-parallel line through a uniform point block, in the coordinate direction\\nnamed by an independent fresh diagonal direction pair at index `i`, together\\nwith that same point.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `i`-th restricted diagonal-line law is the law of the canonical\\ndiagonal line through a uniform point block, in the prefix-projected direction\\nof an independent fresh diagonal direction pair at index `i`, together with\\nthat same point.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineBranch.lean\\n/-!\\n# The branch data of the sub-line sampling procedure\\nThe sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma draws a line kind, a stored\\nextended coordinate, and an unrestricted extended direction, and forms from\\nthem the direction of the extended line: the elementary direction at the\\nstored coordinate in the axis branch, and the extended direction with the\\ncoordinates preceding the stored coordinate replaced by zero in the diagonal\\nbranch.  The two source lines are then read off the two coordinate blocks.\\nThis module fixes the coordinate index that each source line must carry: the\\nstored coordinate itself when it belongs to that block, the first coordinate\\notherwise.  With that choice the source block of the extended direction always\\nlies in the line spanned by the corresponding source direction, which is the\\nincidence hypothesis of the two projection lemmas.  The module also records\\nthat moving an extended point to its canonical representative does not change\\nthe canonical representative of either of its source blocks.\\n## References\\nThe statements support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe coordinate blocks are those of `def:combine-map`, blueprint lines\\n445--480, paper lines 970--989; the prefix truncation is that of\\nblueprint `def:ld-question-distribution`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## The three ranges of extended coordinates -/\\n/-- Every extended coordinate is an `X`-block coordinate, a `Z`-block\\ncoordinate, or one of the two scalar coordinates.  Blueprint\\n`def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-! ## Linearity of the identification of the two scalar fields -/\\n/-- The identification of the two scalar fields is additive on extended\\npoints.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The identification of the two scalar fields carries a scalar multiple of\\nan extended point to the multiple by the identified scalar.  Blueprint\\n`def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-! ## The coordinate index carried by each source line -/\\n/-- The first coordinate of the source space.  Blueprint\\n`eq:chi-func`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:958-961`.\\n-/\\n/-- The coordinate index carried by the source `X` line of a branch: the\\nstored extended coordinate when it is an `X`-block coordinate, and the first\\ncoordinate otherwise.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The coordinate index carried by the source `Z` line of a branch: the\\nstored extended coordinate when it is a `Z`-block coordinate, and the first\\ncoordinate otherwise.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- At an `X`-block coordinate the source `X` line carries that coordinate. -/\\n/-- At a `Z`-block coordinate the source `X` line carries the first\\ncoordinate. -/\\n/-- At the first scalar coordinate the source `X` line carries the first\\ncoordinate. -/\\n/-- At the second scalar coordinate the source `X` line carries the first\\ncoordinate. -/\\n/-- At an `X`-block coordinate the source `Z` line carries the first\\ncoordinate. -/\\n/-- At a `Z`-block coordinate the source `Z` line carries that coordinate. -/\\n/-- At the first scalar coordinate the source `Z` line carries the first\\ncoordinate. -/\\n/-- At the second scalar coordinate the source `Z` line carries the first\\ncoordinate. -/\\n/-! ## The two directions of a branch -/\\n/-- The direction of the extended line of a branch: the elementary direction\\nat the stored coordinate in the axis branch, and the extended direction with\\nthe coordinates preceding the stored coordinate replaced by zero in the\\ndiagonal branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The direction of a source line of a branch: the elementary direction at\\nits coordinate index in the axis branch, and the given source block with the\\ncoordinates preceding that index replaced by zero in the diagonal branch.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Incidence of the two source blocks of a branch direction -/\\n/-- The `X` block of the direction of the extended line of a branch lies in\\nthe line spanned by the direction of its source `X` line.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of the direction of the extended line of a branch lies in\\nthe line spanned by the direction of its source `Z` line.  In the diagonal\\nbranch at an `X`-block coordinate the `Z` block is untruncated, which is why\\nthe source `Z` line carries the first coordinate there.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Canonical representatives of the two source blocks -/\\n/-- Replacing an extended point by its canonical representative in the\\ndirection of the extended line does not change the canonical representative\\nof its `X` block in the direction of the source `X` line, as soon as the `X`\\nblock of the extended direction lies in the line spanned by the source\\ndirection.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Replacing an extended point by its canonical representative in the\\ndirection of the extended line does not change the canonical representative\\nof its `Z` block in the direction of the source `Z` line, as soon as the `Z`\\nblock of the extended direction lies in the line spanned by the source\\ndirection.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineConstruct.lean\\n/-!\\n# The sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma\\nThis module carries out the sampling procedure of `lem:qld-sublines`.  A line\\nkind is drawn from the two kinds, an extended coordinate and an extended point\\nand direction are drawn uniformly, and two scalar seeds are drawn\\nindependently in the coordinate-index fibers of the two coordinates that the\\ntwo source lines must carry.  The sampled triple consists of the axis or\\ndiagonal line of the extended sample together with the two source lines\\ndecoded from the two coordinate blocks of the extended point and direction and\\nfrom the two seeds.\\nThe module records the elementary evaluations of that triple, the description\\nof the support of the resulting law, the three pointwise properties of a\\nsampled triple -- incidence of the two source blocks, compatibility of the\\naffine data, and closure of the axis branch -- and the identification of its\\nextended-line marginal.\\n## References\\nThe construction is the proof of `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe restricted laws are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\n1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\n-/\\n/-! ## The auxiliary randomness of one branch -/\\n/-- An extended point together with an extended direction.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The auxiliary randomness of one branch of the sampling procedure: an\\nextended point, an extended direction, and the two scalar seeds of the two\\nsource lines.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The law of the scalar seed carried by a source line at a given coordinate\\nindex: uniform on the coordinate-index fiber of that index.  Blueprint\\n`def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\n/-- Every seed law of a source line has total mass one. -/\\n/-- The auxiliary randomness law of the sampling procedure: the extended\\ncoordinate is uniform, the extended point and direction are uniform and\\nindependent of it, and the two seeds are independent and uniform on the\\ncoordinate-index fibers of the two coordinates carried by the two source\\nlines.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The auxiliary randomness law has total mass one. -/\\n/-! ## The sampled triple -/\\n/-- The extended line of a branch: the canonical axis or diagonal line of an\\nextended sample.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The extended line of a branch, read off the auxiliary randomness.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- A source line of a branch: the canonical axis or diagonal line decoded\\nfrom a point block, a scalar seed and a direction block.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The triple sampled by one branch of the procedure: the extended line\\ntogether with the two source lines decoded from its two coordinate blocks and\\nfrom the two scalar seeds.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`,\\npaper `references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\n-/\\n/-- The law of the triples sampled by one branch.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The sub-line law of `lem:qld-sublines`: the equal mixture of the two\\nbranches of the sampling procedure.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Elementary evaluations of the sampled triple -/\\n/-- The extended line of a branch has the kind of that branch. -/\\n/-- The direction of the extended line of a branch is the branch direction. -/\\n/-- The base of the extended line of a branch is the canonical representative\\nof the drawn point in the branch direction. -/\\n/-- A source line of a branch has the kind of that branch. -/\\n/-- The direction of a source line of a branch is the branch source direction\\nat the coordinate index named by its seed. -/\\n/-- The base of a source line of a branch is the canonical representative of\\nits point block in its own direction. -/\\n/-! ## The support of the sub-line law -/\\n/-- Every triple in the support of the sub-line law is sampled by one branch\\nfrom auxiliary randomness whose two seeds lie in the coordinate-index fibers\\nof the two coordinates carried by the two source lines.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Pointwise properties of a sampled triple -/\\n/-- The direction of the source `X` line of a sampled triple is the branch\\nsource direction at the coordinate carried by that line. -/\\n/-- The direction of the source `Z` line of a sampled triple is the branch\\nsource direction at the coordinate carried by that line. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of the direction of the extended line of a sampled triple\\nlies in the line spanned by the direction of its source `X` line. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of the direction of the extended line of a sampled triple\\nlies in the line spanned by the direction of its source `Z` line. -/\\n/-- The base of the source `X` line of a sampled triple is the canonical\\nrepresentative, in its own direction, of the `X` block of the base of the\\nextended line.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The base of the source `Z` line of a sampled triple is the canonical\\nrepresentative, in its own direction, of the `Z` block of the base of the\\nextended line.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Every point of the extended line of a sampled triple projects onto both of\\nits source lines.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Every sampled triple carries affine data sufficient for\\n`combineLinePoly_spec`.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- An extended axis line of a sampled triple has axis-parallel source lines.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Total mass and extended marginal -/\\n/-- The sub-line law has total mass one. -/\\n/-- The index-first decomposition of an extended direct sample, read at the\\nbare extended dimension.  This is the decomposition\\n`directLdSpaceIndexEquiv` of `def:ld-question-distribution` with its stored\\ncoordinate typed by the bare dimension `2 m + 2`. -/\\n/-- The extended-line marginal of one branch of the sub-line law is the law of\\nthe canonical line of the corresponding kind through a uniformly sampled\\nextended direct sample.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The extended-line marginal of the sub-line law is the extended-line\\nmarginal of the directly indexed line-point law.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean\\n/-!\\n# Coordinate blocks of an extended point and of an extended direct sample\\nThis module records the block decomposition used by the sampling procedure of\\nthe sub-line lemma on the extended dimension.  A point of the extended space\\nis the tuple consisting of its two source point blocks and its two scalar\\ncoordinates, and this decomposition is a bijection carrying the uniform law to\\nthe uniform law; in particular the two source blocks of a uniform extended\\npoint are independent and uniform.  The decomposition is linear, so it\\ncommutes with the affine parameterization of a line, and it commutes with the\\ncanonical identification of the extended scalar field with the source scalar\\nfield.  The stored coordinate index of an extended direct sample splits along\\nthe same three coordinate ranges.\\n## References\\nThe decompositions support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe coordinate blocks are those of `def:combine-map`, blueprint lines\\n445--480, paper lines 970--989.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Linearity of the coordinate blocks -/\\n/-- The `X` block of a sum of extended points is the sum of the blocks.\\nBlueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of a sum of extended points is the sum of the blocks.\\nBlueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of a scalar multiple of an extended point is the scalar\\nmultiple of the block.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of a scalar multiple of an extended point is the scalar\\nmultiple of the block.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-! ## The coordinate blocks of an extended point -/\\n/-- Split an extended point into its two source point blocks and its two\\nscalar coordinates.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The first block of the decomposition is the `X` block. -/\\n/-- The second block of the decomposition is the `Z` block. -/\\n/-- The first scalar coordinate of the decomposition is `alpha`. -/\\n/-- The second scalar coordinate of the decomposition is `beta`. -/\\n/-- The block decomposition of an extended point carries the uniform law to\\nthe uniform law.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The two source blocks of a uniformly sampled extended point are\\nindependent and uniform.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Transport to the source scalar field -/\\n/-- The `X` block of an extended point transported to the source scalar field\\nis the transport of its `X` block.  Blueprint\\n`def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of an extended point transported to the source scalar field\\nis the transport of its `Z` block.  Blueprint\\n`def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-! ## The blocks of an extended direct sample -/\\n/-- The stored index, point block and direction block of a uniformly sampled\\ndirect low-degree vector are independent and uniform.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Compatibility data read off the coordinate blocks -/\\n/-- An extended line is compatible with two source lines as soon as each of\\nits two point blocks is an affine reparameterization of the corresponding\\nsource line: the base block is a point of the source line and the direction\\nblock is a multiple of the source direction.  The two scalar coordinates of\\nthe compatibility data are the `alpha` and `beta` coordinates of the extended\\nbase and direction.  This supplies the domain condition preceding Equation\\n`eq:combine-lines` in blueprint\\n`def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- An extended line whose two source lines carry the inherited direction\\nblocks is compatible with them, with both affine reparameterizations the\\nidentity.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean\\n/-!\\n# The projected mixtures of the sub-line law\\nThis module assembles the two projected point marginals of the sub-line law of\\n`lem:qld-sublines` into mixtures of the restricted product laws.  Drawing a\\nuniform point of the extended line of a mixture of line laws is the same\\nmixture of the corresponding uniform-point laws, so the two projected\\nmarginals of the sub-line law are the mixtures, over the drawn kind and the\\ndrawn extended coordinate, of the projected marginals of one branch at one\\nextended coordinate.  Each of those is the component law of the pair of\\nindices carried by that branch, so the mixing law is the law of the kind\\ntogether with the pair of indices assigned to a uniform extended coordinate.\\n## References\\nThe assembly is the last step of the proof of `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe restricted laws are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\n1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\n-/\\n/-! ## The uniform point of a mixture of line laws -/\\n/-- Drawing a uniform point of the extended line of a mixture of line-triple\\nlaws is the corresponding mixture of the uniform-point laws.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The projected marginals of one branch at one extended coordinate -/\\n/-- One branch of the sampling procedure, read at a fixed extended coordinate,\\nis the law of the triple decoded from the auxiliary randomness of that\\ncoordinate.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `X` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch at\\none extended coordinate is the `X` component law of the pair of indices of\\nthat branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch at\\none extended coordinate is the `Z` component law of the pair of indices of\\nthat branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The mixing law of the two indices -/\\n/-- The law of the kind and the pair of source indices assigned to a uniform\\nextended coordinate of one branch.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The mixing law of the sub-line lemma: the equal mixture over the two kinds\\nof the law of the pair of source indices of a uniform extended coordinate.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The mixing law has total mass one. -/\\n/-! ## The two projected marginals of one branch -/\\n/-- The `X` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch is\\nthe mixture, along the pair of source indices of a uniform extended\\ncoordinate, of the `X` component laws.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch is\\nthe mixture, along the pair of source indices of a uniform extended\\ncoordinate, of the `Z` component laws.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The two projected marginals of the sub-line law -/\\n/-- The `X` point marginal of the sub-line law is the mixture of the `X`\\ncomponent laws along the mixing law.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` point marginal of the sub-line law is the mixture of the `Z`\\ncomponent laws along the mixing law.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The two one-point projected marginals of the sub-line law are mixtures of\\nthe restricted product laws along one common mixing law.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean\\n/-!\\n# Coordinate blocks of a truncated extended direction\\nThis module computes the two source blocks of the direction of an extended\\ndiagonal line.  That direction is obtained from an unrestricted extended\\nvector by zeroing the coordinates that precede the stored coordinate index,\\nso each source block is again a prefix truncation: when the stored index is\\nthe `i`-th coordinate of a block, that block inherits the truncation at `i`,\\npreceding blocks vanish, and later blocks remain untruncated.\\nThe computation is recorded after transport to the source scalar field, since\\nthe extended direction and the source line directions are presented over the\\ntwo identified scalar fields.\\n## References\\nThe block computations support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe truncation is the prefix restriction of blueprint\\n`def:ld-question-distribution`; the coordinate blocks are those of\\nblueprint `def:combine-map`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Truncation at the first coordinate -/\\n/-- Zeroing the coordinates preceding the first coordinate changes nothing.\\nBlueprint `def:ld-question-distribution`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:31-391`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## The `X` block of a truncated extended direction -/\\n/-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at an `X`-block\\ncoordinate is the `X` block truncated at that coordinate of the source space.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at a `Z`-block\\ncoordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at the first scalar\\ncoordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at the second scalar\\ncoordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The `Z` block of a truncated extended direction -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at a `Z`-block\\ncoordinate is the `Z` block truncated at that coordinate of the source space.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at an `X`-block\\ncoordinate is the untruncated `Z` block, since every `Z`-block coordinate\\nfollows every `X`-block coordinate.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at the first scalar\\ncoordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at the second scalar\\ncoordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean\\n/-!\\n# Products and push-forwards of finite distributions\\nThe sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma presents one law both as an\\nindependent product of its generating blocks and as a dependent mixture over\\none of those blocks.  This module records the three rewriting rules that pass\\nbetween the two presentations: a push-forward of the first factor of a product\\nis a push-forward of the product, a product with a probability law in the\\nsecond factor has the first factor as its marginal, and a push-forward of a\\nproduct is the dependent mixture of the push-forwards of its second factor.\\n## References\\nThe rewriting rules support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe product and mixture operations are those of blueprint\\n`def:line-point-dist`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Nonemptiness of the support of a probability law -/\\n/-- A law of total mass one has a nonempty support.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Push-forward of one factor of a product -/\\n/-- Pushing the first factor of a product forward is pushing the product\\nforward along the map acting on that factor alone.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The first marginal of a product -/\\n/-- The first marginal of a product whose second factor is a probability law\\nis the first factor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Reading a function of the first factor of a product whose second factor is\\na probability law is reading it on the first factor.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## A product as a dependent mixture -/\\n/-- A push-forward of a product is the dependent mixture, over the first\\nfactor, of the push-forwards of the second factor.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/drx/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr400/79caf482f3bb07bf4392bccacf0f2384adf8fb8f/prose-last-message.md.dispatch.log:75:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_37\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"awk 'NR >= 3975 && NR < 5698 { if (/\\\"'^diff --git/) print; if (/''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\+[\\\"'^+]/) {s=substr($0,2); if(s ~ /''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\/-/) comment=1; if(comment) print s; if(s ~ /-\\\\\\\\//) comment=0} }' /home/drx/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr400/79caf482f3bb07bf4392bccacf0f2384adf8fb8f/diff.patch\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"diff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean\\n/-!\\n# The two independent blocks of a fresh diagonal direction pair\\nThis module splits the fresh diagonal direction pair of the sub-line sampling\\nprocedure into its two independent blocks.  The coordinate-index event\\nconstrains only the scalar seed of the pair, so conditioning on it leaves the\\ndirection block uniform and independent of the conditioned seed.  The\\ndirection block of a fresh pair is therefore available as an unconstrained\\nuniform vector, which is what allows the sampling procedure to read it off the\\ncorresponding block of the direction of the sampled extended line.\\n## References\\nThe block decomposition supports `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe coordinate-index event is that of `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint\\nlines 1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Conditioning the first factor of a product -/\\n/-- Restricting a product law to an event depending on the first factor alone\\nleaves the second factor unchanged and independent of the conditioned first\\nfactor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Formalization-only auxiliary: the mass of an event depending only on the\\nfirst factor is the same under a uniform product law and under the uniform\\nlaw of that factor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Conditioning a uniform product law on an event of its first factor leaves\\nthe second factor uniform and independent.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The coordinate-index event on a scalar seed -/\\n/-- The coordinate-index event of `def:ith-restricted-line` read on a scalar\\nseed alone.  Blueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\n/-- Decidability of the coordinate-index event on a scalar seed. -/\\n/-- Every coordinate-index event of a scalar seed has positive mass under the\\nuniform law.  Blueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\n/-! ## The blocks of a fresh diagonal direction pair -/\\n/-- A fresh diagonal direction pair at coordinate index `i` is a scalar seed\\nconditioned on the coordinate-index fiber of `i` together with an independent\\nuniform direction block.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The direction block of a fresh diagonal direction pair is uniform.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean\\n/-!\\n# Reshuffling products of finitely supported laws\\nThe sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma produces its six independent\\ngenerating blocks in one grouping, while the restricted line-point laws that\\nthe projected marginals must match present the same six blocks in another\\ngrouping.  This module records the rewriting rules that pass between two such\\npresentations: the support and the weight of a product, the fact that a\\npush-forward along a bijection is determined by the supports and weights it\\ncompares, and the push-forward of the second factor of a product.\\n## References\\nThe rewriting rules support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe product operation is that of blueprint\\n`def:line-point-dist`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## The support and the weight of a product -/\\n/-- The support of a product law is the product of the supports.  Blueprint\\n`def:line-point-dist`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:274-287`.\\n-/\\n/-- The weight of a product law is the product of the weights.  Blueprint\\n`def:line-point-dist`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:274-287`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Push-forward along a bijection -/\\n/-- A push-forward along a bijection is identified by the supports and the\\nweights it compares: if the map matches the two supports and carries each\\nweight to the corresponding weight, and if it has a two-sided inverse, the\\npush-forward is the target law.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Push-forward of the second factor of a product -/\\n/-- Pushing the second factor of a product forward is pushing the product\\nforward along the map acting on that factor alone.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean\\n/-!\\n# The projected marginals of the sub-line law\\nThis module identifies the two projected point marginals of the sub-line law\\nof `lem:qld-sublines` as mixtures of the restricted product laws.  The two\\nsource lines of a sampled triple depend on the drawn extended point only\\nthrough the canonical representatives of its two source blocks, and those\\nrepresentatives are unchanged when the drawn point is replaced by any point of\\nthe extended line; a uniform point of the extended line through a uniform\\npoint is a uniform point, so the drawn point may be replaced throughout by the\\npoint that is finally retained.  The six generating blocks of the resulting\\nlaw -- the two source point blocks, the two scalar seeds and the two source\\ndirection blocks -- are then regrouped into the two restricted product laws\\ncarried by the two indices of the branch.\\n## References\\nThe identification is the last step of the proof of `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe restricted laws are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\n1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Source lines depend on the point block only through its representative -/\\n/-- Two source lines of the same branch with the same seed and the same source\\ndirection block agree as soon as their point blocks have the same canonical\\nrepresentative in the direction of that line.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Replacing the drawn extended point by a point of the extended line does\\nnot change the canonical representative of its `X` block in the direction of\\nthe source `X` line of the branch.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Replacing the drawn extended point by a point of the extended line does\\nnot change the canonical representative of its `Z` block in the direction of\\nthe source `Z` line of the branch.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Generating form of the restricted product components -/\\n/-- The restricted line-point law of a kind at an index is the law of the\\nsource line of that kind decoded from a uniform point block, a scalar seed in\\nthe coordinate-index fiber of the index and a uniform direction block,\\ntogether with that same point block.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `X` component law of a pair of indices is the law of the two source\\nlines decoded from two independent restricted blocks, together with the point\\nblock of the first.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` component law of a pair of indices is the law of the two source\\nlines decoded from two independent restricted blocks, together with the point\\nblock of the second.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The joint projected marginal of one branch at one extended coordinate -/\\n/-- At a fixed line kind and a fixed extended coordinate, the joint law of the\\ntwo source lines of a sampled triple together with the two source blocks of a\\nuniform point of its extended line is the law of the two source lines decoded\\nfrom two independent restricted blocks together with their two point blocks.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The two one-point projected marginals of one branch -/\\n/-- At a fixed line kind and a fixed extended coordinate, the joint law of the\\ntwo source lines of a sampled triple together with the source `X` block of a\\nuniform point of its extended line is the `X` component law of the two indices\\nof that branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- At a fixed line kind and a fixed extended coordinate, the joint law of the\\ntwo source lines of a sampled triple together with the source `Z` block of a\\nuniform point of its extended line is the `Z` component law of the two indices\\nof that branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSupport.lean\\n/-!\\n# Generating laws of the restricted line-point distributions\\nThis module rewrites the two restricted line-point laws of\\n`def:ith-restricted-line` as push-forwards of a single uniformly sampled\\nlow-degree vector conditioned on the coordinate-index fiber of its retained\\nscalar seed.  In that form the point block, the seed and the direction block\\nof the sampled vector are visibly the independent generating data of the\\nrestricted law, which is the description used by the sampling procedure of the\\nsub-line lemma: there the projected pairs are built either from a direction\\nblock inherited from the extended line or from freshly sampled data, and each\\nis asserted to follow a restricted law.\\n## References\\nThe generating descriptions support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe restricted laws themselves are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\n1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Restriction of a push-forward -/\\n/-- Formalization-only auxiliary: restricting a push-forward distribution to a\\ndecidable event is the push-forward of the restriction to the pre-image of\\nthat event.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The coordinate-index event on a single uniform vector -/\\n/-- The coordinate-index event of `def:ith-restricted-line` read on the single\\nuniformly sampled low-degree vector that generates the line-point laws.\\nBlueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\n/-- Decidability of the coordinate-index event. -/\\n/-- The coordinate-index event has positive mass under the uniform law on\\nlow-degree vectors, so the conditioning below is normalized.  Blueprint\\n`def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\n/-! ## Canonical representatives are unchanged by the conditioning maps -/\\n/-- Two axis-line descriptions with the same seed and the same base agree:\\nthe stored proof of `def:line-representative` invariance is a proposition and\\ncarries no further data.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Two diagonal-line descriptions with the same seed, the same base and the\\nsame direction agree: the stored proofs of `def:line-representative`\\ninvariance and of the prefix-zero condition are propositions and carry no\\nfurther data.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The axis-line decoder is unchanged by the axis conditioning map: the\\ncanonical representative of `def:line-representative` is idempotent, so\\ndecoding the conditioned vector and decoding the original vector agree.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The diagonal-line decoder is unchanged by the diagonal conditioning map:\\nthe prefix projection and the canonical representative of\\n`def:line-representative` are both idempotent.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Generating laws of the restricted line-point distributions -/\\n/-- The `i`-th restricted axis-line law is the law of the pair consisting of\\nthe canonical axis-parallel line through a low-degree vector and its point\\nblock, when that vector is uniform conditioned on the coordinate-index fiber\\nof its seed.  This is the generating description used in the sampling\\nprocedure of blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `i`-th restricted diagonal-line law is the law of the pair consisting\\nof the canonical diagonal line through a low-degree vector and its point\\nblock, when that vector is uniform conditioned on the coordinate-index fiber\\nof its seed.  This is the generating description used in the sampling\\nprocedure of blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineTransport.lean\\n/-!\\n# Uniformity under the identification of the two scalar fields\\nThe extended line of the sub-line construction lives over the scalar field of\\nthe directly indexed extended game, while the two source lines live over the\\nscalar field of the Pauli basis test.  The two fields are identified\\ncoordinatewise by a field isomorphism, so that identification is a bijection\\nof the coordinate spaces and therefore carries uniform laws to uniform laws.\\nThis module records that transport for a whole extended point and for the two\\nsource blocks of an extended point, and it records the resampling rule that\\nreading a function at the point of a uniformly random affine parameter on the\\nline through the canonical representative of a uniformly random point is the\\nsame as reading that function at a uniformly random point.\\n## References\\nThe statements support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe coordinate blocks are those of `def:combine-map`, blueprint lines\\n445--480, paper lines 970--989.\\n-/\\n/-! ## The identification of the two scalar fields as a bijection -/\\n/-- The coordinatewise identification of the extended scalar field with the\\nsource scalar field on one source block, presented as a bijection.  Blueprint\\n`def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The coordinatewise identification of the extended scalar field with the\\nsource scalar field on a whole extended point, presented as a bijection.\\nBlueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of a transported extended point is the transport of its `X`\\nblock.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of a transported extended point is the transport of its `Z`\\nblock.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-! ## Transport of the uniform laws -/\\n/-- A uniformly random extended point transported to the source scalar field\\nis uniformly random.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- A uniformly random source block transported to the source scalar field is\\nuniformly random.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The two source blocks of a uniformly random extended point, read in the\\nsource scalar field, are independent and uniformly random.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Resampling a uniform point along a line -/\\n/-- Reading a function at the point of a uniformly random affine parameter on\\nthe line through the canonical representative, in a fixed direction, of a\\nuniformly random point is the same as reading that function at a uniformly\\nrandom point.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineUniform.lean\\n/-!\\n# Uniform points of a line and the blocks of a coordinate direction\\nThis module records the two elementary geometric inputs of the sampling\\nprocedure of the sub-line lemma. First, for independent uniform inputs `y` in\\nthe ambient coordinate space and `t` in the scalar field, the point\\n`lineRepMap v y + t • v` is uniform. This map on ambient-point--parameter pairs\\nhas fibers of size `q`: for nonzero `v`, `y` varies over a coset of the span of\\n`v`, while its canonical representative and `t` are fixed; for zero `v`, `y`\\nis fixed and `t` is free. Second, the two coordinate\\nblocks of an elementary coordinate direction of the extended space are the\\ncorresponding elementary direction of the source space in the block that\\ncarries the coordinate, and zero in the other block and at the two scalar\\ncoordinates.\\n## References\\nThe two statements support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe canonical representative map is blueprint\\n`def:line-representative`; the coordinate blocks are those of\\nblueprint `def:combine-map`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## The canonical representative for the zero direction -/\\n/-- For the zero direction the canonical representative map of\\n`def:line-representative` is the identity, since the span of the zero vector\\nis trivial.  Blueprint `def:line-representative`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:143-174`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Uniform points of a line -/\\n/-- The point at a uniformly random affine parameter on the line through the\\ncanonical representative of a uniformly random point is uniformly random on\\nthe whole coordinate space.  Every fiber of the parameterization has exactly\\n`q` elements in the ambient-point--parameter domain: for a nonzero direction\\nthe parameter and canonical representative are determined, while the input\\npoint varies over a coset of the span of the direction. For the zero direction\\nthe input point is fixed and the parameter is free. Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Injectivity of the two block embeddings -/\\n/-- The `X`-block coordinate embedding of `def:combine-map` is injective.\\nBlueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z`-block coordinate embedding of `def:combine-map` is injective.\\nBlueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-! ## The blocks of an elementary coordinate direction -/\\n/-- The `X` block of the elementary direction at an `X`-block coordinate is\\nthe elementary direction of the source space at that coordinate.  Blueprint\\n`def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of the elementary direction at a `Z`-block coordinate\\nvanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of the elementary direction at the first scalar coordinate\\nvanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of the elementary direction at the second scalar coordinate\\nvanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of the elementary direction at a `Z`-block coordinate is\\nthe elementary direction of the source space at that coordinate.  Blueprint\\n`def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of the elementary direction at an `X`-block coordinate\\nvanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of the elementary direction at the first scalar coordinate\\nvanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of the elementary direction at the second scalar coordinate\\nvanishes.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-! ## The coordinates occupied by the two blocks -/\\n/-- The `i`-th `X`-block coordinate of the extended space is its `i`-th\\ncoordinate.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `i`-th `Z`-block coordinate of the extended space is its `(m + i)`-th\\ncoordinate.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The extended coordinate carrying the first scalar is the coordinate\\n`2 m`.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The extended coordinate carrying the second scalar is the coordinate\\n`2 m + 1`.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-! ## The blocks of a point of an extended line -/\\n/-- Transporting the extended scalar field to the source scalar field carries\\na point of a line to a point of the transported line, since the transport is\\na coordinatewise field isomorphism.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of a point of a line lies on the source line through the\\ncanonical representative of the `X` block of the base, as soon as the `X`\\nblock of the direction is a multiple of the source direction.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of a point of a line lies on the source line through the\\ncanonical representative of the `Z` block of the base, as soon as the `Z`\\nblock of the direction is a multiple of the source direction.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Incidence of the blocks of an extended line -/\\n/-- The identification of the extended scalar field with the source scalar\\nfield carries an elementary coordinate direction to the elementary coordinate\\ndirection at the same coordinate.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Incidence of the `X` blocks: if a source line has as base the canonical\\nrepresentative, in its own direction, of the `X` block of the base of an\\nextended line, and if the `X` block of the direction of the extended line\\nlies in the span of the source direction, then the `X` block of every point\\nof the extended line is a point of the source line.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Incidence of the `Z` blocks: if a source line has as base the canonical\\nrepresentative, in its own direction, of the `Z` block of the base of an\\nextended line, and if the `Z` block of the direction of the extended line\\nlies in the span of the source direction, then the `Z` block of every point\\nof the extended line is a point of the source line.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/drx/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr400/79caf482f3bb07bf4392bccacf0f2384adf8fb8f/prose-last-message.md.dispatch.log:81:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_40\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"awk 'NR >= 3168 && NR < 3975 { if (/\\\"'^diff --git/) print; if (/''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\+[\\\"'^+]/) {s=substr($0,2); if(s ~ /''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\/-/) comment=1; if(comment) print s; if(s ~ /-\\\\\\\\//) comment=0} }' /home/drx/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr400/79caf482f3bb07bf4392bccacf0f2384adf8fb8f/diff.patch\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"diff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineExtended.lean\\n/-!\\n# Coordinate blocks of an extended point and of an extended direct sample\\nThis module records the block decomposition used by the sampling procedure of\\nthe sub-line lemma on the extended dimension.  A point of the extended space\\nis the tuple consisting of its two source point blocks and its two scalar\\ncoordinates, and this decomposition is a bijection carrying the uniform law to\\nthe uniform law; in particular the two source blocks of a uniform extended\\npoint are independent and uniform.  The decomposition is linear, so it\\ncommutes with the affine parameterization of a line, and it commutes with the\\ncanonical identification of the extended scalar field with the source scalar\\nfield.  The stored coordinate index of an extended direct sample splits along\\nthe same three coordinate ranges.\\n## References\\nThe decompositions support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe coordinate blocks are those of `def:combine-map`, blueprint lines\\n445--480, paper lines 970--989.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Linearity of the coordinate blocks -/\\n/-- The `X` block of a sum of extended points is the sum of the blocks.\\nBlueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of a sum of extended points is the sum of the blocks.\\nBlueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of a scalar multiple of an extended point is the scalar\\nmultiple of the block.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of a scalar multiple of an extended point is the scalar\\nmultiple of the block.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-! ## The coordinate blocks of an extended point -/\\n/-- Split an extended point into its two source point blocks and its two\\nscalar coordinates.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The first block of the decomposition is the `X` block. -/\\n/-- The second block of the decomposition is the `Z` block. -/\\n/-- The first scalar coordinate of the decomposition is `alpha`. -/\\n/-- The second scalar coordinate of the decomposition is `beta`. -/\\n/-- The block decomposition of an extended point carries the uniform law to\\nthe uniform law.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The two source blocks of a uniformly sampled extended point are\\nindependent and uniform.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Transport to the source scalar field -/\\n/-- The `X` block of an extended point transported to the source scalar field\\nis the transport of its `X` block.  Blueprint\\n`def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of an extended point transported to the source scalar field\\nis the transport of its `Z` block.  Blueprint\\n`def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-! ## The blocks of an extended direct sample -/\\n/-- The stored index, point block and direction block of a uniformly sampled\\ndirect low-degree vector are independent and uniform.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Compatibility data read off the coordinate blocks -/\\n/-- An extended line is compatible with two source lines as soon as each of\\nits two point blocks is an affine reparameterization of the corresponding\\nsource line: the base block is a point of the source line and the direction\\nblock is a multiple of the source direction.  The two scalar coordinates of\\nthe compatibility data are the `alpha` and `beta` coordinates of the extended\\nbase and direction.  This supplies the domain condition preceding Equation\\n`eq:combine-lines` in blueprint\\n`def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\n/-- An extended line whose two source lines carry the inherited direction\\nblocks is compatible with them, with both affine reparameterizations the\\nidentity.  Blueprint `def:combine-map`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:970-989`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineMixture.lean\\n/-!\\n# The projected mixtures of the sub-line law\\nThis module assembles the two projected point marginals of the sub-line law of\\n`lem:qld-sublines` into mixtures of the restricted product laws.  Drawing a\\nuniform point of the extended line of a mixture of line laws is the same\\nmixture of the corresponding uniform-point laws, so the two projected\\nmarginals of the sub-line law are the mixtures, over the drawn kind and the\\ndrawn extended coordinate, of the projected marginals of one branch at one\\nextended coordinate.  Each of those is the component law of the pair of\\nindices carried by that branch, so the mixing law is the law of the kind\\ntogether with the pair of indices assigned to a uniform extended coordinate.\\n## References\\nThe assembly is the last step of the proof of `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe restricted laws are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\n1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\n-/\\n/-! ## The uniform point of a mixture of line laws -/\\n/-- Drawing a uniform point of the extended line of a mixture of line-triple\\nlaws is the corresponding mixture of the uniform-point laws.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The projected marginals of one branch at one extended coordinate -/\\n/-- One branch of the sampling procedure, read at a fixed extended coordinate,\\nis the law of the triple decoded from the auxiliary randomness of that\\ncoordinate.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `X` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch at\\none extended coordinate is the `X` component law of the pair of indices of\\nthat branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch at\\none extended coordinate is the `Z` component law of the pair of indices of\\nthat branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The mixing law of the two indices -/\\n/-- The law of the kind and the pair of source indices assigned to a uniform\\nextended coordinate of one branch.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The mixing law of the sub-line lemma: the equal mixture over the two kinds\\nof the law of the pair of source indices of a uniform extended coordinate.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The mixing law has total mass one. -/\\n/-! ## The two projected marginals of one branch -/\\n/-- The `X` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch is\\nthe mixture, along the pair of source indices of a uniform extended\\ncoordinate, of the `X` component laws.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` marginal of a uniform point of the extended line of one branch is\\nthe mixture, along the pair of source indices of a uniform extended\\ncoordinate, of the `Z` component laws.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The two projected marginals of the sub-line law -/\\n/-- The `X` point marginal of the sub-line law is the mixture of the `X`\\ncomponent laws along the mixing law.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` point marginal of the sub-line law is the mixture of the `Z`\\ncomponent laws along the mixing law.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The two one-point projected marginals of the sub-line law are mixtures of\\nthe restricted product laws along one common mixing law.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLinePrefix.lean\\n/-!\\n# Coordinate blocks of a truncated extended direction\\nThis module computes the two source blocks of the direction of an extended\\ndiagonal line.  That direction is obtained from an unrestricted extended\\nvector by zeroing the coordinates that precede the stored coordinate index,\\nso each source block is again a prefix truncation: when the stored index is\\nthe `i`-th coordinate of a block, that block inherits the truncation at `i`,\\npreceding blocks vanish, and later blocks remain untruncated.\\nThe computation is recorded after transport to the source scalar field, since\\nthe extended direction and the source line directions are presented over the\\ntwo identified scalar fields.\\n## References\\nThe block computations support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe truncation is the prefix restriction of blueprint\\n`def:ld-question-distribution`; the coordinate blocks are those of\\nblueprint `def:combine-map`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Truncation at the first coordinate -/\\n/-- Zeroing the coordinates preceding the first coordinate changes nothing.\\nBlueprint `def:ld-question-distribution`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:31-391`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## The `X` block of a truncated extended direction -/\\n/-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at an `X`-block\\ncoordinate is the `X` block truncated at that coordinate of the source space.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at a `Z`-block\\ncoordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at the first scalar\\ncoordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `X` block of an extended direction truncated at the second scalar\\ncoordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The `Z` block of a truncated extended direction -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at a `Z`-block\\ncoordinate is the `Z` block truncated at that coordinate of the source space.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at an `X`-block\\ncoordinate is the untruncated `Z` block, since every `Z`-block coordinate\\nfollows every `X`-block coordinate.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at the first scalar\\ncoordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` block of an extended direction truncated at the second scalar\\ncoordinate vanishes.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineProduct.lean\\n/-!\\n# Products and push-forwards of finite distributions\\nThe sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma presents one law both as an\\nindependent product of its generating blocks and as a dependent mixture over\\none of those blocks.  This module records the three rewriting rules that pass\\nbetween the two presentations: a push-forward of the first factor of a product\\nis a push-forward of the product, a product with a probability law in the\\nsecond factor has the first factor as its marginal, and a push-forward of a\\nproduct is the dependent mixture of the push-forwards of its second factor.\\n## References\\nThe rewriting rules support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe product and mixture operations are those of blueprint\\n`def:line-point-dist`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Nonemptiness of the support of a probability law -/\\n/-- A law of total mass one has a nonempty support.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Push-forward of one factor of a product -/\\n/-- Pushing the first factor of a product forward is pushing the product\\nforward along the map acting on that factor alone.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The first marginal of a product -/\\n/-- The first marginal of a product whose second factor is a probability law\\nis the first factor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Reading a function of the first factor of a product whose second factor is\\na probability law is reading it on the first factor.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## A product as a dependent mixture -/\\n/-- A push-forward of a product is the dependent mixture, over the first\\nfactor, of the push-forwards of the second factor.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/drx/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr400/79caf482f3bb07bf4392bccacf0f2384adf8fb8f/prose-last-message.md.dispatch.log:83:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_41\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"awk 'NR >= 3975 && NR < 4921 { if (/\\\"'^diff --git/) print; if (/''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\+[\\\"'^+]/) {s=substr($0,2); if(s ~ /''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\/-/) comment=1; if(comment) print s; if(s ~ /-\\\\\\\\//) comment=0} }' /home/drx/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr400/79caf482f3bb07bf4392bccacf0f2384adf8fb8f/diff.patch\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"diff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSeed.lean\\n/-!\\n# The two independent blocks of a fresh diagonal direction pair\\nThis module splits the fresh diagonal direction pair of the sub-line sampling\\nprocedure into its two independent blocks.  The coordinate-index event\\nconstrains only the scalar seed of the pair, so conditioning on it leaves the\\ndirection block uniform and independent of the conditioned seed.  The\\ndirection block of a fresh pair is therefore available as an unconstrained\\nuniform vector, which is what allows the sampling procedure to read it off the\\ncorresponding block of the direction of the sampled extended line.\\n## References\\nThe block decomposition supports `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe coordinate-index event is that of `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint\\nlines 1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Conditioning the first factor of a product -/\\n/-- Restricting a product law to an event depending on the first factor alone\\nleaves the second factor unchanged and independent of the conditioned first\\nfactor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Formalization-only auxiliary: the mass of an event depending only on the\\nfirst factor is the same under a uniform product law and under the uniform\\nlaw of that factor.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Conditioning a uniform product law on an event of its first factor leaves\\nthe second factor uniform and independent.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The coordinate-index event on a scalar seed -/\\n/-- The coordinate-index event of `def:ith-restricted-line` read on a scalar\\nseed alone.  Blueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\n/-- Decidability of the coordinate-index event on a scalar seed. -/\\n/-- Every coordinate-index event of a scalar seed has positive mass under the\\nuniform law.  Blueprint `def:ith-restricted-line`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1038-1048`. -/\\n/-! ## The blocks of a fresh diagonal direction pair -/\\n/-- A fresh diagonal direction pair at coordinate index `i` is a scalar seed\\nconditioned on the coordinate-index fiber of `i` together with an independent\\nuniform direction block.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The direction block of a fresh diagonal direction pair is uniform.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineShuffle.lean\\n/-!\\n# Reshuffling products of finitely supported laws\\nThe sampling procedure of the sub-line lemma produces its six independent\\ngenerating blocks in one grouping, while the restricted line-point laws that\\nthe projected marginals must match present the same six blocks in another\\ngrouping.  This module records the rewriting rules that pass between two such\\npresentations: the support and the weight of a product, the fact that a\\npush-forward along a bijection is determined by the supports and weights it\\ncompares, and the push-forward of the second factor of a product.\\n## References\\nThe rewriting rules support `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe product operation is that of blueprint\\n`def:line-point-dist`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## The support and the weight of a product -/\\n/-- The support of a product law is the product of the supports.  Blueprint\\n`def:line-point-dist`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:274-287`.\\n-/\\n/-- The weight of a product law is the product of the weights.  Blueprint\\n`def:line-point-dist`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:274-287`.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Push-forward along a bijection -/\\n/-- A push-forward along a bijection is identified by the supports and the\\nweights it compares: if the map matches the two supports and carries each\\nweight to the corresponding weight, and if it has a two-sided inverse, the\\npush-forward is the target law.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Push-forward of the second factor of a product -/\\n/-- Pushing the second factor of a product forward is pushing the product\\nforward along the map acting on that factor alone.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\ndiff --git a/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean b/MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Lines/SubLineSource.lean\\n/-!\\n# The projected marginals of the sub-line law\\nThis module identifies the two projected point marginals of the sub-line law\\nof `lem:qld-sublines` as mixtures of the restricted product laws.  The two\\nsource lines of a sampled triple depend on the drawn extended point only\\nthrough the canonical representatives of its two source blocks, and those\\nrepresentatives are unchanged when the drawn point is replaced by any point of\\nthe extended line; a uniform point of the extended line through a uniform\\npoint is a uniform point, so the drawn point may be replaced throughout by the\\npoint that is finally retained.  The six generating blocks of the resulting\\nlaw -- the two source point blocks, the two scalar seeds and the two source\\ndirection blocks -- are then regrouped into the two restricted product laws\\ncarried by the two indices of the branch.\\n## References\\nThe identification is the last step of the proof of `lem:qld-sublines` in\\nblueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, whose source is\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`.\\nThe restricted laws are `def:ith-restricted-line`, blueprint lines\\n1209--1228, paper lines 1038--1048.\\n-/\\n/-! ## Source lines depend on the point block only through its representative -/\\n/-- Two source lines of the same branch with the same seed and the same source\\ndirection block agree as soon as their point blocks have the same canonical\\nrepresentative in the direction of that line.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Replacing the drawn extended point by a point of the extended line does\\nnot change the canonical representative of its `X` block in the direction of\\nthe source `X` line of the branch.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- Replacing the drawn extended point by a point of the extended line does\\nnot change the canonical representative of its `Z` block in the direction of\\nthe source `Z` line of the branch.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## Generating form of the restricted product components -/\\n/-- The restricted line-point law of a kind at an index is the law of the\\nsource line of that kind decoded from a uniform point block, a scalar seed in\\nthe coordinate-index fiber of the index and a uniform direction block,\\ntogether with that same point block.  Blueprint\\n`lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `X` component law of a pair of indices is the law of the two source\\nlines decoded from two independent restricted blocks, together with the point\\nblock of the first.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- The `Z` component law of a pair of indices is the law of the two source\\nlines decoded from two independent restricted blocks, together with the point\\nblock of the second.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The joint projected marginal of one branch at one extended coordinate -/\\n/-- At a fixed line kind and a fixed extended coordinate, the joint law of the\\ntwo source lines of a sampled triple together with the two source blocks of a\\nuniform point of its extended line is the law of the two source lines decoded\\nfrom two independent restricted blocks together with their two point blocks.\\nBlueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-! ## The two one-point projected marginals of one branch -/\\n/-- At a fixed line kind and a fixed extended coordinate, the joint law of the\\ntwo source lines of a sampled triple together with the source `X` block of a\\nuniform point of its extended line is the `X` component law of the two indices\\nof that branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n/-- At a fixed line kind and a fixed extended coordinate, the joint law of the\\ntwo source lines of a sampled triple together with the source `Z` block of a\\nuniform point of its extended line is the `Z` component law of the two indices\\nof that branch.  Blueprint `lem:qld-sublines`, paper\\n`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1063-1116`. -/\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/drx/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr400/79caf482f3bb07bf4392bccacf0f2384adf8fb8f/prose-last-message.md.dispatch.log:91:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_45\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/ZEvalDeficit.lean'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\timport MIPStarRE.QPBT.Combining.Lines\\n     2\\timport MIPStarRE.QPBT.Combining.OrderedPoints\\n     3\\timport MIPStarRE.QPBT.Combining.OverlapGap\\n     4\\timport MIPStarRE.QPBT.Combining.Points.PlacementSupport\\n     5\\t\\n     6\\t/-!\\n     7\\t# Z-point deficits of the evaluated pair-line measurement\\n     8\\t\\n     9\\tThe proof of `lem:claim-17-3` bounds, after its Cauchy--Schwarz step, the\\n    10\\tdeficit of the overlap between the evaluated pair-line measurement and the\\n    11\\texpanded `Z`-point measurement at the `Z`-point of the sample.\\n    12\\tThis module derives that deficit from the\\n    13\\tconsistency of the evaluated pair-line measurement with the completed joint\\n    14\\tpoint measurement and from the closeness of the joint point measurement to the\\n    15\\tordered point products. The derivation inserts the joint point measurement\\n    16\\ton the point side, replaces it by the ordered product whose trailing factor is\\n    17\\tthe retained point effect, and sums the other factor, at the cost of three\\n    18\\tsquare roots. The deficit is first bounded over an arbitrary probability\\n    19\\tlaw on pairs of line-point pairs and then over a product of two restricted\\n    20\\tline-point laws, whose point marginal is the uniform pair.\\n    21\\tThe `_at` variants retain arbitrary opposite placements, including Bob at\\n    22\\t`BBprime` and Alice at `ABdoubleprime`. The original statements are unchanged\\n    23\\tspecializations; register commutation does not assume symmetry of the state.\\n    24\\t\\n    25\\t## References\\n    26\\t\\n    27\\tThe Z deficit is the quantity bounded in the proof of `lem:claim-17-3`\\n    28\\tin `blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex`, paper\\n    29\\t`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1204-1239`; the\\n    30\\tconsistency input is `lem:qld-xz-lines` and the ordered products are those of\\n    31\\t`lem:qld-4-10`.\\n    32\\t-/\\n    33\\t\\n    34\\topen scoped BigOperators Matrix MatrixOrder ComplexOrder\\n    35\\t\\n    36\\tnamespace MIPStarRE.QPBT\\n    37\\t\\n    38\\topen MIPStarRE.LDT hiding Measurement\\n    39\\topen MIPStarRE.Quantum MIPStarRE.QPBT.DistanceCalculus\\n    40\\t\\n    41\\tnoncomputable section\\n    42\\t\\n    43\\t/-! ## Completed point pairs -/\\n    44\\t\\n    45\\t/-- A sum over completed outcome pairs of a function vanishing on both `none`\\n    46\\tclasses is the sum over the uncompleted pairs. -/\\n    47\\ttheorem sum_option_prod_eq_of_none {β γ : Type*} [Fintype β] [Fintype γ]\\n    48\\t    (F : Option β × Option γ → ℝ) (h1 : ∀ o2, F (none, o2) = 0)\\n    49\\t    (h2 : ∀ o1, F (o1, none) = 0) :\\n    50\\t    (∑ o, F o) = ∑ bc : β × γ, F (some bc.1, some bc.2) := by\\n    51\\t  calc (∑ o : Option β × Option γ, F o)\\n    52\\t      = ∑ o1 : Option β, ∑ o2 : Option γ, F (o1, o2) := Fintype.sum_prod_type (f := F)\\n    53\\t    _ = (∑ o2 : Option γ, F (none, o2)) +\\n    54\\t          ∑ b : β, ∑ o2 : Option γ, F (some b, o2) := Fintype.sum_option _\\n    55\\t    _ = ∑ b : β, ∑ o2 : Option γ, F (some b, o2) := by\\n    56\\t        rw [Finset.sum_eq_zero fun o2 _ => h1 o2, zero_add]\\n    57\\t    _ = ∑ b : β, (F (some b, none) + ∑ c : γ, F (some b, some c)) :=\\n    58\\t        Finset.sum_congr rfl fun b _ => Fintype.sum_option _\\n    59\\t    _ = ∑ b : β, ∑ c : γ, F (some b, some c) := by\\n    60\\t        refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun b _ => ?_\\n    61\\t        rw [h2 (some b), zero_add]\\n    62\\t    _ = ∑ bc : β × γ, F (some bc.1, some bc.2) :=\\n    63\\t        (Fintype.sum_prod_type (f := fun bc : β × γ => F (some bc.1, some bc.2))).symm\\n    64\\t\\n    65\\t/-- The completed joint measurement has the joint effect on a pair of\\n    66\\tevaluations. -/\\n    67\\ttheorem completedPair_effect_some {β γ d : Type*} [Fintype β] [Fintype γ]\\n    68\\t    [DecidableEq β] [DecidableEq γ] [Fintype d] [DecidableEq d]\\n    69\\t    (Q : Measurement (β × γ) d) (bc : β × γ) :\\n    70\\t    (Q.postprocess fun ab => (some ab.1, some ab.2)).effect (some bc.1, some bc.2) =\\n    71\\t      Q.effect bc := by\\n    72\\t  rw [MIPStarRE.Quantum.Measurement.postprocess_effect,\\n    73\\t    show (Finset.univ.filter fun cd : β × γ =>\\n    74\\t        (some cd.1, some cd.2) = (some bc.1, some bc.2)) = {bc} by\\n    75\\t      ext cd; simp [Prod.ext_iff, eq_comm]]\\n    76\\t  simp\\n    77\\t\\n    78\\t/-- The completed joint measurement vanishes on outcomes outside the image of\\n    79\\tthe completion. -/\\n    80\\ttheorem completedPair_effect_eq_zero {β γ d : Type*} [Fintype β] [Fintype γ]\\n    81\\t    [DecidableEq β] [DecidableEq γ] [Fintype d] [DecidableEq d]\\n    82\\t    (Q : Measurement (β × γ) d) (o : Option β × Option γ)\\n    83\\t    (ho : ∀ bc : β × γ, (some bc.1, some bc.2) ≠ o) :\\n    84\\t    (Q.postprocess fun ab => (some ab.1, some ab.2)).effect o = 0 := by\\n    85\\t  rw [MIPStarRE.Quantum.Measurement.postprocess_effect]\\n    86\\t  exact Finset.sum_eq_zero fun bc hbc =>\\n    87\\t    absurd (Finset.mem_filter.mp hbc).2 (ho bc)\\n    88\\t\\n    89\\t/-- The completion of a projective joint measurement is projective. -/\\n    90\\ttheorem completedPair_isProjective {β γ d : Type*} [Fintype β] [Fintype γ]\\n    91\\t    [DecidableEq β] [DecidableEq γ] [Fintype d] [DecidableEq d]\\n    92\\t    (Q : Measurement (β × γ) d) (hQ : Measurement.IsProjective Q) :\\n    93\\t    Measurement.IsProjective (Q.postprocess fun ab => (some ab.1, some ab.2)) := by\\n    94\\t  intro o\\n    95\\t  by_cases h : ∃ bc : β × γ, (some bc.1, some bc.2) = o\\n    96\\t  · obtain ⟨bc, rfl⟩ := h\\n    97\\t    rw [completedPair_effect_some]\\n    98\\t    exact hQ bc\\n    99\\t  · push Not at h\\n   100\\t    rw [completedPair_effect_eq_zero Q o h]\\n   101\\t    exact IsStarProjection.zero _\\n   102\\t\\n   103\\tnamespace ProjectiveSetting\\n   104\\t\\n   105\\tvariable {P : AdmissibleParams} {ε : ℝ}\\n   106\\t\\n   107\\t/-- A placed projection is a projection. -/\\n   108\\ttheorem place_isProj (S : ProjectiveSetting P ε) (p : Placement)\\n   109\\t    {E : Op (S.ExpandedLocalSpace p.side)} (hE : IsProj E) :\\n   110\\t    IsProj (S.place p E) := by\\n   111\\t  refine isStarProjection_iff'.2 ⟨?_, ?_⟩\\n   112\\t  · change S.place p E * S.place p E = S.place p E\\n   113\\t    rw [← place_mul, hE.isIdempotentElem.eq]\\n   114\\t  · rw [Matrix.star_eq_conjTranspose, ← place_conjTranspose,\\n   115\\t      hE.isSelfAdjoint.isHermitian.eq]\\n   116\\t\\n   117\\t/-- Every effect of the completed expanded point measurement is a projection,\\n   118\\tthe `none` effect being zero. -/\\n   119\\ttheorem pointMeasExpOption_isProj (S : ProjectiveSetting P ε)\\n   120\\t    (side : PlayerSide) (W : PauliKind) (u : Fin P.m → PauliScalar P)\\n   121\\t    (o : Option (PauliScalar P)) :\\n   122\\t    IsProj ((S.pointMeasExpOption side W u).effect o) := by\\n   123\\t  cases o with\\n   124\\t  | none =>\\n   125\\t    rw [S.pointMeasExpOption_effect_none]\\n   126\\t    exact IsStarProjection.zero _\\n   127\\t  | some a =>\\n   128\\t    rw [S.pointMeasExpOption_effect_some]\\n   129\\t    exact S.pointMeasExp_isProjective side W u a\\n   130\\t\\n   131\\t/-- The completed joint point measurement and a completed point product differ\\n   132\\tonly on outcome pairs with two evaluations, so their placed state-dependent\\n   133\\tsquared distance is the one of the joint point measurement and the point\\n   134\\tproduct.  Blueprint `ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:2651-2698`. -/\\n   135\\ttheorem sum_norm_place_completedPair_sub_sq (S : ProjectiveSetting P ε)\\n   136\\t    (p : Placement)\\n   137\\t    (Q : Measurement (PauliScalar P × PauliScalar P) (S.ExpandedLocalSpace p.side))\\n   138\\t    (Gc : Option (PauliScalar P) × Option (PauliScalar P) →\\n   139\\t      Op (S.ExpandedLocalSpace p.side))\\n   140\\t    (G : PauliScalar P × PauliScalar P → Op (S.ExpandedLocalSpace p.side))\\n   141\\t    (hsome : ∀ ab : PauliScalar P × PauliScalar P, Gc (some ab.1, some ab.2) = G ab)\\n   142\\t    (hnone1 : ∀ o2, Gc (none, o2) = 0) (hnone2 : ∀ o1, Gc (o1, none) = 0) :\\n   143\\t    (∑ o : Option (PauliScalar P) × Option (PauliScalar P),\\n   144\\t        ‖applyOperatorToState\\n   145\\t          (S.place p ((Q.postprocess fun ab => (some ab.1, some ab.2)).effect o) -\\n   146\\t            S.place p (Gc o)) S.psiHat‖ ^ 2) =\\n   147\\t      ∑ ab : PauliScalar P × PauliScalar P,\\n   148\\t        ‖applyOperatorToState (S.place p (Q.effect ab) - S.place p (G ab))\\n   149\\t          S.psiHat‖ ^ 2 := by\\n   150\\t  refine Eq.trans (sum_option_prod_eq_of_none _ ?_ ?_) ?_\\n   151\\t  · intro o2\\n   152\\t    rw [completedPair_effect_eq_zero Q (none, o2) (fun ab => by simp), hnone1,\\n   153\\t      place_zero, sub_self]\\n   154\\t    simp [applyOperatorToState]\\n   155\\t  · intro o1\\n   156\\t    rw [completedPair_effect_eq_zero Q (o1, none) (fun ab => by simp), hnone2,\\n   157\\t      place_zero, sub_self]\\n   158\\t    simp [applyOperatorToState]\\n   159\\t  · refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun ab _ => ?_\\n   160\\t    rw [completedPair_effect_some, hsome]\\n   161\\t\\n   162\\t/-- The completed `Z`-then-`X` point product vanishes on both `none` classes\\n   163\\tand restricts to the ordered product on evaluated pairs. -/\\n   164\\ttheorem completedPair_norm_sq_sum_ZX (S : ProjectiveSetting P ε) {δ : ℝ}\\n   165\\t    (points : CombinedPointsWitness S δ) (p : Placement)\\n   166\\t    (x z : Fin P.m → PauliScalar P) :\\n   167\\t    (∑ o : Option (PauliScalar P) × Option (PauliScalar P),\\n   168\\t        ‖applyOperatorToState\\n   169\\t          (S.place p (((points.Q p.side x z).postprocess (fun ab =>\\n   170\\t              (some ab.1, some ab.2))).effect o) -\\n   171\\t            S.place p\\n   172\\t              ((S.pointMeasExpOption p.side .Z z).effect o.2 *\\n   173\\t                (S.pointMeasExpOption p.side .X x).effect o.1))\\n   174\\t          S.psiHat‖ ^ 2) =\\n   175\\t      ∑ ab : PauliScalar P × PauliScalar P,\\n   176\\t        ‖applyOperatorToState\\n   177\\t          (S.place p ((points.Q p.side x z).effect ab) -\\n   178\\t            S.place p\\n   179\\t              ((S.pointMeasExp p.side .Z z).effect ab.2 *\\n   180\\t                (S.pointMeasExp p.side .X x).effect ab.1))\\n   181\\t          S.psiHat‖ ^ 2 := by\\n   182\\t  refine S.sum_norm_place_completedPair_sub_sq p (points.Q p.side x z) _ _ ?_ ?_ ?_\\n   183\\t  · intro ab\\n   184\\t    rw [S.pointMeasExpOption_effect_some, S.pointMeasExpOption_effect_some]\\n   185\\t  · intro o2\\n   186\\t    rw [S.pointMeasExpOption_effect_none, mul_zero]\\n   187\\t  · intro o1\\n   188\\t    rw [S.pointMeasExpOption_effect_none, zero_mul]\\n   189\\t\\n   190\\t\\n   191\\tend ProjectiveSetting\\n   192\\t\\n   193\\t/-! ## The abstract one-point deficit -/\\n   194\\t\\n   195\\t/-- Let `A` be a complete measurement, `Q` a projective measurement, `R` a\\n   196\\tfamily of projections and `W` a family of operators, all indexed by the same\\n   197\\toutcomes, with `Q`, `R` and `W` commuting with the effects of `A` and with\\n   198\\t`W R = W`.  If `A` and `Q` are consistent with defect at most `η` and `Q` is\\n   199\\twithin squared distance `ζ` of `Wᴴ`, then the deficit of the overlap of `A`\\n   200\\twith `R` is at most `2 √η + 2 √ζ`.  The proof inserts `Q` before `R`, replaces\\n   201\\t`Q R` by `W R = W`, and compares `W` with `Q`; each of the three steps is a\\n   202\\tCauchy--Schwarz estimate weighted by `A`.  This is the common estimate behind\\n   203\\tthe deficits in the proofs of `lem:claim-17-2` and `lem:claim-17-3`, paper\\n   204\\t`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1168-1239`. -/\\n   205\\ttheorem one_sub_overlap_le_of_consistencyDefect {X α ι : Type*}\\n   206\\t    [Fintype X] [DecidableEq X] [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\\n   207\\t    [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι]\\n   208\\t    (μ : Distribution X) (A Q : X → Measurement α ι) (R W : X → α → Op ι)\\n   209\\t    (ψ : EuclideanSpace ℂ ι) (hμ : μ.IsProbability) (hψ : ‖ψ‖ = 1)\\n   210\\t    (hQ : ∀ x, Measurement.IsProjective (Q x)) (hR : ∀ x a, IsProj (R x a))\\n   211\\t    (hAQ : ∀ x a, Commute ((A x).effect a) ((Q x).effect a))\\n   212\\t    (hAR : ∀ x a, Commute ((A x).effect a) (R x a))\\n   213\\t    (hAW : ∀ x a, Commute ((A x).effect a) (W x a))\\n   214\\t    (hWR : ∀ x a, W x a * R x a = W x a)\\n   215\\t    (hnonneg : ∀ x a, 0 ≤ stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * (Q x).effect a))\\n   216\\t    (η ζ : ℝ)\\n   217\\t    (hcons : consistencyDefect μ (fun x a => (A x).effect a)\\n   218\\t      (fun x a => (Q x).effect a) ψ ≤ η)\\n   219\\t    (hdist : opFamilyDistSq μ (fun x a => (Q x).effect a)\\n   220\\t      (fun x a => (W x a)ᴴ) ψ ≤ ζ) :\\n   221\\t    1 - avgOver μ (fun x => ∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * R x a)) ≤\\n   222\\t      2 * Real.sqrt η + 2 * Real.sqrt ζ := by\\n   223\\t  set ovQ := avgOver μ (fun x =>\\n   224\\t    ∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * (Q x).effect a)) with hovQ\\n   225\\t  have h1 : 1 - ovQ ≤ η := by\\n   226\\t    rw [← consistencyDefect_eq_one_sub_overlap μ A Q ψ hμ hψ]\\n   227\\t    exact hcons\\n   228\\t  have hovQ_nonneg : 0 ≤ ovQ :=\\n   229\\t    avgOver_nonneg μ _ fun x => Finset.sum_nonneg fun a _ => hnonneg x a\\n   230\\t  have hovQ_le : ovQ ≤ 1 :=\\n   231\\t    avgOver_sum_stateQForm_mul_le_one μ A (fun x a => (Q x).effect a) ψ hμ hψ\\n   232\\t      (fun x a => hQ x a) hAQ\\n   233\\t  have h1' : 1 - ovQ ≤ Real.sqrt η := by\\n   234\\t    have h0 : 0 ≤ 1 - ovQ := by linarith\\n   235\\t    calc 1 - ovQ = Real.sqrt ((1 - ovQ) ^ 2) := (Real.sqrt_sq h0).symm\\n   236\\t      _ ≤ Real.sqrt η := Real.sqrt_le_sqrt (by nlinarith)\\n   237\\t  -- comparing `Q` with `W` under the weight `A`\\n   238\\t  have hWconj : ∀ x a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * W x a) =\\n   239\\t      stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * (W x a)ᴴ) := by\\n   240\\t    intro x a\\n   241\\t    rw [← stateQForm_conjTranspose ψ ((A x).effect a * W x a),\\n   242\\t      Matrix.conjTranspose_mul, measurement_effect_hermitian]\\n   243\\t    congr 1\\n   244\\t    have h := congrArg Matrix.conjTranspose (hAW x a).eq\\n   245\\t    rw [Matrix.conjTranspose_mul, Matrix.conjTranspose_mul,\\n   246\\t      measurement_effect_hermitian] at h\\n   247\\t    exact h\\n   248\\t  have h2 : |ovQ - avgOver μ (fun x =>\\n   249\\t      ∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * W x a))| ≤ Real.sqrt ζ := by\\n   250\\t    rw [avgOver_congr μ _ _ fun x => Finset.sum_congr rfl fun a _ => hWconj x a]\\n   251\\t    exact abs_overlap_gap_le_sqrt_of_opFamilyDistSq μ A\\n   252\\t      (fun x a => (Q x).effect a) (fun x a => (W x a)ᴴ) ψ hμ hψ ζ hdist\\n   253\\t  -- replacing `Q R` by `W R = W`\\n   254\\t  have h4 : |avgOver μ (fun x =>\\n   255\\t      ∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * ((Q x).effect a * R x a))) -\\n   256\\t        avgOver μ (fun x => ∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * W x a))| ≤\\n   257\\t      Real.sqrt ζ := by\\n   258\\t    have hpt : ∀ x,\\n   259\\t        (∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * ((Q x).effect a * R x a))) -\\n   260\\t          (∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * W x a)) =\\n   261\\t        ∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * (((Q x).effect a - W x a) * R x a)) := by\\n   262\\t      intro x\\n   263\\t      rw [← Finset.sum_sub_distrib]\\n   264\\t      refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun a _ => ?_\\n   265\\t      rw [sub_mul, hWR]\\n   266\\t      simp [stateQForm, applyOperatorToState, mul_sub]\\n   267\\t    rw [← avgOver_sub, avgOver_congr μ _ _ hpt]\\n   268\\t    have hcs := abs_avgOver_sum_stateQForm_mul_le_sqrt_of_isProj' μ A\\n   269\\t      (fun x a => (Q x).effect a - W x a) R ψ hμ hψ hR hAR\\n   270\\t      (fun x a => (hAQ x a).sub_right (hAW x a))\\n   271\\t    refine hcs.trans (Real.sqrt_le_sqrt ?_)\\n   272\\t    have hdist' : avgOver μ (fun x =>\\n   273\\t        ∑ a, ‖applyOperatorToState ((Q x).effect a - (W x a)ᴴ) ψ‖ ^ 2) ≤ ζ :=\\n   274\\t      hdist\\n   275\\t    refine le_of_eq_of_le ?_ hdist'\\n   276\\t    refine avgOver_congr μ _ _ fun x => Finset.sum_congr rfl fun a _ => ?_\\n   277\\t    rw [Matrix.conjTranspose_sub, measurement_effect_hermitian]\\n   278\\t  -- inserting `Q` before `R`\\n   279\\t  have h5 : |avgOver μ (fun x =>\\n   280\\t      ∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * ((Q x).effect a * R x a))) -\\n   281\\t        avgOver μ (fun x => ∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * R x a))| ≤\\n   282\\t      Real.sqrt η := by\\n   283\\t    have hpt : ∀ x,\\n   284\\t        (∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * R x a)) -\\n   285\\t          (∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * ((Q x).effect a * R x a))) =\\n   286\\t        ∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * ((1 - (Q x).effect a) * R x a)) := by\\n   287\\t      intro x\\n   288\\t      rw [← Finset.sum_sub_distrib]\\n   289\\t      refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun a _ => ?_\\n   290\\t      simp [stateQForm, applyOperatorToState, sub_mul, mul_sub]\\n   291\\t    rw [abs_sub_comm, ← avgOver_sub, avgOver_congr μ _ _ hpt]\\n   292\\t    have hcs := abs_avgOver_sum_stateQForm_mul_mul_le_sqrt_mul_sqrt μ A\\n   293\\t      (fun x a => 1 - (Q x).effect a) R ψ (fun x a => (hQ x a).one_sub)\\n   294\\t      (fun x a => (Commute.one_right _).sub_right (hAQ x a))\\n   295\\t    have hfirst : avgOver μ (fun x =>\\n   296\\t        ∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * (1 - (Q x).effect a))) = 1 - ovQ := by\\n   297\\t      have hx : ∀ x,\\n   298\\t          (∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * (1 - (Q x).effect a))) =\\n   299\\t            1 - ∑ a, stateQForm ψ ((A x).effect a * (Q x).effect a) := by\\n   300\\t        intro x\\n   301\\t        rw [← sum_stateQForm_effect_eq_one (A x) ψ hψ, ← Finset.sum_sub_distrib]\\n   302\\t        refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun a _ => ?_\\n   303\\t        simp [stateQForm, applyOperatorToState, mul_sub]\\n   304\\t      rw [avgOver_congr μ _ _ hx, avgOver_sub, avgOver_const_of_isProbability μ hμ]\\n   305\\t    have hsecond : avgOver μ (fun x =>\\n   306\\t        ∑ a, stateQForm (applyOperatorToState (R x a) ψ) ((A x).effect a)) ≤ 1 := by\\n   307\\t      rw [avgOver_congr μ _ _ fun x => Finset.sum_congr rfl fun a _ =>\\n   308\\t        stateQForm_applyOperatorToState_eq_of_isProj ψ (hR x a) (hAR x a)]\\n   309\\t      exact avgOver_sum_stateQForm_mul_le_one μ A R ψ hμ hψ hR hAR\\n   310\\t    rw [hfirst] at hcs\\n   311\\t    calc\\n   312\\t      _ ≤ _ := hcs\\n   313\\t      _ ≤ Real.sqrt (1 - ovQ) * 1 := by\\n   314\\t        refine mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left ?_ (Real.sqrt_nonneg _)\\n   315\\t        rw [← Real.sqrt_one]\\n   316\\t        exact Real.sqrt_le_sqrt hsecond\\n   317\\t      _ = Real.sqrt (1 - ovQ) := mul_one _\\n   318\\t      _ ≤ Real.sqrt η := Real.sqrt_le_sqrt h1\\n   319\\t  have h2' := abs_le.mp h2\\n   320\\t  have h4' := abs_le.mp h4\\n   321\\t  have h5' := abs_le.mp h5\\n   322\\t  linarith [h2'.1, h4'.1, h5'.1, h2'.2, h4'.2, h5'.2]\\n   323\\t\\n   324\\t/-! ## The two placed deficits -/\\n   325\\t\\n   326\\t/-- A pair of line-point pairs of the source space: the sample type of the\\n   327\\tconsistency display of `lem:qld-xz-lines`. -/\\n   328\\tabbrev LinePointPairSample (P : AdmissibleParams) :=\\n   329\\t  (LineDesc P.toLdParams × (Fin P.m → PauliScalar P)) ×\\n   330\\t    (LineDesc P.toLdParams × (Fin P.m → PauliScalar P))\\n   331\\t\\n   332\\tsection Placed\\n   333\\t\\n   334\\tvariable {P : AdmissibleParams} {ε δQ δP : ℝ} {S : ProjectiveSetting P ε}\\n   335\\t  {points : CombinedPointsWitness S δQ}\\n   336\\t\\n   337\\t/-- The `Z`-point deficit of the evaluated pair-line measurement over a\\n   338\\tprobability law on pairs of line-point pairs, from the consistency of the\\n   339\\tevaluated pair-line measurement with the completed joint point measurement\\n   340\\tand the closeness of the latter to the `Z`-then-`X` product.  This is the\\n   341\\tdeficit bounded in the proof of `lem:claim-17-3`, paper\\n   342\\t`references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1204-1239`. -/\\n   343\\ttheorem CombinedLinesWitness.one_sub_Z_overlap_le_at\\n   344\\t    (lines : CombinedLinesWitness S points δP)\\n   345\\t    (first second : Placement) (hopposite : first.IsOpposite second)\\n   346\\t    (ν : Distribution (LinePointPairSample P)) (hν : ν.IsProbability) (η ζ : ℝ)\\n   347\\t    (hcons : consistencyDefect ν\\n   348\\t      (fun s o => S.place first\\n   349\\t        (((lines.T first.side s.1.1 s.2.1).postprocess fun fs =>\\n   350\\t          (evalOpt s.1.1 s.1.2 fs.1, evalOpt s.2.1 s.2.2 fs.2)).effect o))\\n   351\\t      (fun s o => S.place second\\n   352\\t        (((points.Q second.side s.1.2 s.2.2).postprocess fun ab =>\\n   353\\t          (some ab.1, some ab.2)).effect o))\\n   354\\t      S.psiHat ≤ η)\\n   355\\t    (hZX : opFamilyDistSq ν\\n   356\\t      (fun s o => S.place second\\n   357\\t        (((points.Q second.side s.1.2 s.2.2).postprocess fun ab =>\\n   358\\t          (some ab.1, some ab.2)).effect o))\\n   359\\t      (fun s o => S.place second\\n   360\\t        ((S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2 *\\n   361\\t          (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .X s.1.2).effect o.1))\\n   362\\t      S.psiHat ≤ ζ) :\\n   363\\t    1 - avgOver ν (fun s =>\\n   364\\t      ∑ o : Option (PauliScalar P) × Option (PauliScalar P),\\n   365\\t        stateQForm S.psiHat\\n   366\\t          (S.place first\\n   367\\t            (((lines.T first.side s.1.1 s.2.1).postprocess fun fs =>\\n   368\\t              (evalOpt s.1.1 s.1.2 fs.1, evalOpt s.2.1 s.2.2 fs.2)).effect o) *\\n   369\\t            S.place second ((S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2))) ≤\\n   370\\t      2 * Real.sqrt η + 2 * Real.sqrt ζ := by\\n   371\\t  have hQproj : ∀ s : LinePointPairSample P,\\n   372\\t      Measurement.IsProjective (S.placedMeasurement second\\n   373\\t        ((points.Q second.side s.1.2 s.2.2).postprocess fun ab => (some ab.1, some ab.2))) :=\\n   374\\t    fun s => S.placedMeasurement_isProjective _ _\\n   375\\t      (completedPair_isProjective _ (points.projective _ _ _))\\n   376\\t  have hRproj : ∀ (s : LinePointPairSample P)\\n   377\\t      (o : Option (PauliScalar P) × Option (PauliScalar P)),\\n   378\\t      IsProj (S.place second ((S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2)) :=\\n   379\\t    fun s o => S.place_isProj _ (S.pointMeasExpOption_isProj _ _ _ _)\\n   380\\t  have hWR : ∀ (s : LinePointPairSample P)\\n   381\\t      (o : Option (PauliScalar P) × Option (PauliScalar P)),\\n   382\\t      S.place second ((S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .X s.1.2).effect o.1 *\\n   383\\t          (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2) *\\n   384\\t        S.place second ((S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2) =\\n   385\\t      S.place second ((S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .X s.1.2).effect o.1 *\\n   386\\t        (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2) := by\\n   387\\t    intro s o\\n   388\\t    have hlocal : (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .X s.1.2).effect o.1 *\\n   389\\t          (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2 *\\n   390\\t        (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2 =\\n   391\\t        (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .X s.1.2).effect o.1 *\\n   392\\t          (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2 := by\\n   393\\t      rw [mul_assoc, (S.pointMeasExpOption_isProj second.side .Z s.2.2 o.2).isIdempotentElem.eq]\\n   394\\t    exact (ProjectiveSetting.place_mul S second _ _).symm.trans\\n   395\\t      (congrArg (S.place second) hlocal)\\n   396\\t  have hnonneg : ∀ (s : LinePointPairSample P)\\n   397\\t      (o : Option (PauliScalar P) × Option (PauliScalar P)),\\n   398\\t      0 ≤ stateQForm S.psiHat\\n   399\\t        (S.place first\\n   400\\t          (((lines.T first.side s.1.1 s.2.1).postprocess fun fs =>\\n   401\\t            (evalOpt s.1.1 s.1.2 fs.1, evalOpt s.2.1 s.2.2 fs.2)).effect o) *\\n   402\\t          S.place second\\n   403\\t            (((points.Q second.side s.1.2 s.2.2).postprocess fun ab =>\\n   404\\t              (some ab.1, some ab.2)).effect o)) :=\\n   405\\t    fun s o => stateQForm_nonneg _ (S.place_mul_place_nonneg first second hopposite\\n   406\\t      (((lines.T first.side s.1.1 s.2.1).postprocess fun fs =>\\n   407\\t        (evalOpt s.1.1 s.1.2 fs.1, evalOpt s.2.1 s.2.2 fs.2)).pos o)\\n   408\\t      (((points.Q second.side s.1.2 s.2.2).postprocess fun ab =>\\n   409\\t        (some ab.1, some ab.2)).pos o))\\n   410\\t  have hdist : opFamilyDistSq ν\\n   411\\t      (fun s o => S.place second\\n   412\\t        (((points.Q second.side s.1.2 s.2.2).postprocess fun ab =>\\n   413\\t          (some ab.1, some ab.2)).effect o))\\n   414\\t      (fun s o => (S.place second\\n   415\\t        ((S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .X s.1.2).effect o.1 *\\n   416\\t          (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2))ᴴ)\\n   417\\t      S.psiHat ≤ ζ := by\\n   418\\t    refine le_of_eq_of_le ?_ hZX\\n   419\\t    unfold opFamilyDistSq\\n   420\\t    refine avgOver_congr _ _ _ fun s => Finset.sum_congr rfl fun o _ => ?_\\n   421\\t    have hlocal : ((S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .X s.1.2).effect o.1 *\\n   422\\t          (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2)ᴴ =\\n   423\\t        (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .Z s.2.2).effect o.2 *\\n   424\\t          (S.pointMeasExpOption second.side .X s.1.2).effect o.1 := by\\n   425\\t      rw [Matrix.conjTranspose_mul,\\n   426\\t        (S.po